WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:01.216 --> 00:03.196 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference November 3,

00:03.196 --> 00:04.436 align:middle
2021 CHAIR POWELL.

00:04.546 --> 00:04.856 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:04.856 --> 00:07.116 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:07.116 --> 00:09.106 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:09.106 --> 00:10.386 align:middle
that Congress has given us:

00:11.156 --> 00:13.376 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

00:14.306 --> 00:18.136 align:middle
Today, the FOMC kept
interest rates near zero and,

00:18.136 --> 00:20.716 align:middle
in light of the progress the
economy has made toward our

00:20.716 --> 00:24.736 align:middle
goals, decided to begin reducing
the pace of asset purchases.

00:25.766 --> 00:28.596 align:middle
With these actions,
monetary policy will continue

00:28.596 --> 00:31.316 align:middle
to provide strong support
to the economic recovery.

00:32.746 --> 00:35.466 align:middle
Given the unprecedented nature
of the disruptions related

00:35.466 --> 00:37.866 align:middle
to the pandemic and the
reopening of the economy,

00:38.456 --> 00:40.936 align:middle
we remain attentive to
risks and will ensure

00:40.936 --> 00:42.756 align:middle
that our policy is
well positioned

00:43.336 --> 00:46.676 align:middle
to address the full range of
plausible economic outcomes.

00:46.766 --> 00:50.746 align:middle
I will say more about our
monetary policy decisions

00:50.886 --> 00:53.236 align:middle
after reviewing recent
economic developments.

00:55.016 --> 00:59.036 align:middle
Economic activity expanded
at a 6.5 percent pace

00:59.036 --> 01:01.956 align:middle
in the first half of the
year, reflecting progress

01:01.956 --> 01:04.536 align:middle
on vaccinations, the
reopening of the economy,

01:05.236 --> 01:06.626 align:middle
and strong policy support.

01:07.136 --> 01:11.836 align:middle
In the third quarter, real
GDP growth slowed notably

01:11.836 --> 01:13.106 align:middle
from this rapid pace.

01:13.676 --> 01:15.776 align:middle
The summer's surge
in COVID cases

01:15.776 --> 01:18.326 align:middle
from the Delta variant
has held back the recovery

01:18.616 --> 01:21.486 align:middle
in the sectors most adversely
affected by the pandemic,

01:21.976 --> 01:23.536 align:middle
including travel and leisure.

01:24.506 --> 01:27.866 align:middle
Activity has also been
restrained by supply constraints

01:27.866 --> 01:31.106 align:middle
and bottlenecks, notably in
the motor vehicle industry.

01:31.106 --> 01:34.206 align:middle
As a result, both
household spending

01:34.206 --> 01:36.686 align:middle
and business investment
flattened out last quarter.

01:37.786 --> 01:40.876 align:middle
Nonetheless, aggregate demand
has been very strong this year,

01:41.496 --> 01:44.546 align:middle
buoyed by fiscal and
monetary policy support

01:44.586 --> 01:46.206 align:middle
and the healthy financial
positions

01:46.206 --> 01:47.786 align:middle
of households and businesses.

01:48.286 --> 01:53.186 align:middle
With COVID case counts
receding further and progress

01:53.186 --> 01:56.736 align:middle
on vaccinations, economic growth
should pick up this quarter,

01:56.906 --> 01:59.736 align:middle
resulting in strong growth
for the year as a whole.

02:01.276 --> 02:03.866 align:middle
Conditions in the labor market
have continued to improve,

02:03.866 --> 02:06.266 align:middle
and demand for workers
remains very strong.

02:07.536 --> 02:09.486 align:middle
As with overall economic
activity,

02:09.626 --> 02:13.106 align:middle
the pace of improvement slowed
with the rise in COVID cases.

02:14.056 --> 02:15.176 align:middle
In August and September,

02:15.476 --> 02:20.576 align:middle
job gains averaged 280,000 per
month, down from an average

02:20.576 --> 02:23.506 align:middle
of about 1 million jobs
per month in June and July.

02:24.096 --> 02:26.906 align:middle
The slowdown has
been concentrated

02:26.906 --> 02:31.016 align:middle
in sectors most sensitive to
the pandemic, including leisure

02:31.016 --> 02:32.706 align:middle
and hospitality and education.

02:34.146 --> 02:37.486 align:middle
The unemployment rate was
4.8 percent in September.

02:38.516 --> 02:40.946 align:middle
This figure understates the
shortfall in employment,

02:41.126 --> 02:42.916 align:middle
particularly as participation

02:42.916 --> 02:44.776 align:middle
in the labor market
remains subdued.

02:46.126 --> 02:47.036 align:middle
Some of the softness

02:47.036 --> 02:49.336 align:middle
in participation likely
reflects the aging

02:49.336 --> 02:51.106 align:middle
of the population
and retirements.

02:52.106 --> 02:55.846 align:middle
But participation for prime-aged
individuals also remains well

02:55.846 --> 03:00.076 align:middle
below pre-pandemic levels-in
part reflecting factors related

03:00.076 --> 03:02.566 align:middle
to the pandemic, such
as caregiving needs

03:02.936 --> 03:05.246 align:middle
and ongoing concerns
about the virus.

03:05.246 --> 03:09.296 align:middle
As a result, employers are
having difficulties filling

03:09.296 --> 03:10.176 align:middle
job openings.

03:11.206 --> 03:14.086 align:middle
These impediments to labor
supply should diminish

03:14.086 --> 03:15.186 align:middle
with further progress

03:15.186 --> 03:17.856 align:middle
on containing the
virus-supporting gains

03:17.856 --> 03:19.666 align:middle
in employment and
economic activity.

03:20.236 --> 03:25.036 align:middle
The economic downturn has not
fallen equally on all Americans,

03:25.176 --> 03:27.456 align:middle
and those least able to
shoulder the burden have been

03:27.456 --> 03:28.166 align:middle
hardest hit.

03:29.206 --> 03:31.236 align:middle
Despite progress,
joblessness continues

03:31.236 --> 03:34.596 align:middle
to fall disproportionately on
African Americans and Hispanics.

03:35.956 --> 03:39.376 align:middle
The supply and demand imbalances
related to the pandemic

03:39.376 --> 03:42.256 align:middle
and the reopening of the
economy have contributed

03:42.256 --> 03:44.826 align:middle
to sizable price
increases in some sectors.

03:45.836 --> 03:47.446 align:middle
In particular, bottlenecks

03:47.446 --> 03:51.406 align:middle
and supply chain disruptions are
limiting how quickly production

03:51.406 --> 03:54.426 align:middle
can respond to the rebound
in demand in the near term.

03:55.286 --> 03:58.206 align:middle
As a result, overall
inflation is running well

03:58.206 --> 04:00.216 align:middle
above our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

04:01.436 --> 04:02.976 align:middle
Supply constraints
have been larger

04:02.976 --> 04:04.796 align:middle
and longer lasting
than anticipated.

04:05.696 --> 04:08.526 align:middle
Nonetheless, it remains
the case that the drivers

04:08.526 --> 04:11.626 align:middle
of higher inflation have
been predominantly connected

04:12.186 --> 04:15.776 align:middle
to the dislocations caused
by the pandemic-specifically,

04:15.776 --> 04:18.316 align:middle
the effects on supply and
demand from the shutdown,

04:18.606 --> 04:21.226 align:middle
the uneven reopening,
and the ongoing effects

04:21.286 --> 04:22.636 align:middle
of the virus itself.

04:23.136 --> 04:27.186 align:middle
We understand the difficulties
that high inflation poses

04:27.186 --> 04:29.536 align:middle
for individuals and
families, particularly those

04:29.536 --> 04:33.016 align:middle
with limited means to absorb
higher prices for essentials

04:33.116 --> 04:34.786 align:middle
such as food and transportation.

04:35.316 --> 04:39.026 align:middle
Our tools cannot ease
supply constraints.

04:39.266 --> 04:41.526 align:middle
Like most forecasters,
we continue to believe

04:41.526 --> 04:44.636 align:middle
that our dynamic economy
will adjust to the supply

04:44.636 --> 04:47.176 align:middle
and demand imbalances
and that, as it does,

04:47.266 --> 04:49.706 align:middle
inflation will decline
to levels much closer

04:49.706 --> 04:51.896 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

04:52.766 --> 04:55.556 align:middle
Of course, it is very difficult
to predict the persistence

04:55.556 --> 04:58.186 align:middle
of supply constraints or
their effects on inflation.

04:59.166 --> 05:02.556 align:middle
Global supply chains are
complex; they will return

05:02.556 --> 05:04.276 align:middle
to normal function,
but the timing

05:04.276 --> 05:05.656 align:middle
of that is highly uncertain.

05:05.656 --> 05:09.436 align:middle
We are committed to
our longer-run goal

05:09.436 --> 05:10.676 align:middle
of 2 percent inflation

05:10.916 --> 05:13.916 align:middle
and to having longer-term
inflation expectations well

05:13.916 --> 05:15.116 align:middle
anchored at this goal.

05:15.936 --> 05:18.206 align:middle
If we were to see signs
that the path of inflation,

05:18.516 --> 05:20.456 align:middle
or [of] longer-term
inflation expectations,

05:20.936 --> 05:22.216 align:middle
was moving materially

05:22.216 --> 05:24.736 align:middle
and persistently beyond levels
consistent with our goal,

05:25.346 --> 05:27.716 align:middle
we would use our tools to
preserve price stability.

05:29.006 --> 05:30.216 align:middle
We will be watching carefully

05:30.216 --> 05:32.136 align:middle
to see whether the
economy is evolving

05:32.136 --> 05:33.576 align:middle
in line with expectations.

05:35.856 --> 05:39.336 align:middle
The Fed's policy actions have
been guided by our mandate

05:39.336 --> 05:41.596 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

05:41.596 --> 05:44.606 align:middle
for the American people, along
with our responsibilities

05:44.606 --> 05:46.686 align:middle
to promote the stability
of the financial system.

05:47.806 --> 05:49.916 align:middle
Our asset purchases have
been a critical tool.

05:50.416 --> 05:53.406 align:middle
They helped preserve financial
stability early in the pandemic

05:53.686 --> 05:56.536 align:middle
and since then have helped
foster smooth market functioning

05:56.796 --> 05:58.426 align:middle
and accommodative
financial conditions

05:58.426 --> 06:01.286 align:middle
to support the economy.

06:01.386 --> 06:04.816 align:middle
Last December, the
Committee stated its intention

06:04.816 --> 06:07.136 align:middle
to continue asset
purchases at a pace

06:07.136 --> 06:09.666 align:middle
of at least $120
billion per month

06:10.136 --> 06:14.356 align:middle
until substantial further
progress has been made toward

06:14.356 --> 06:17.026 align:middle
our maximum-employment
and price-stability goals.

06:17.676 --> 06:19.976 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee judged

06:19.976 --> 06:21.766 align:middle
that the economy
has met this test,

06:22.316 --> 06:25.386 align:middle
and decided to begin reducing
the pace of its asset purchases.

06:26.206 --> 06:29.746 align:middle
Beginning later this month, we
will reduce the monthly pace

06:29.836 --> 06:32.986 align:middle
of our net asset
purchases by $10 billion

06:32.986 --> 06:35.406 align:middle
for Treasury securities
and $5 billion

06:35.406 --> 06:37.186 align:middle
for agency mortgage-backed
securities.

06:38.206 --> 06:40.806 align:middle
We also announced another
reduction of this size

06:40.906 --> 06:43.976 align:middle
in the monthly purchase pace
starting in mid-December,

06:44.186 --> 06:46.826 align:middle
since that month's purchase
schedule will be released

06:46.866 --> 06:48.966 align:middle
by the Federal Reserve
Bank of New York prior

06:48.966 --> 06:50.566 align:middle
to our December FOMC meeting.

06:51.626 --> 06:54.016 align:middle
If the economy evolves
broadly as expected,

06:54.686 --> 06:57.336 align:middle
we judge that similar
reductions in the pace

06:57.336 --> 07:00.306 align:middle
of net asset purchases will
likely be appropriate each

07:00.306 --> 07:02.456 align:middle
month, implying that increases

07:02.456 --> 07:04.146 align:middle
in our securities
holdings would cease

07:04.146 --> 07:05.396 align:middle
by the middle of next year.

07:06.316 --> 07:09.506 align:middle
That said, we are prepared to
adjust the pace of purchases

07:09.506 --> 07:11.976 align:middle
if warranted by changes
in the economic outlook.

07:13.396 --> 07:16.576 align:middle
And even after our balance sheet
stops expanding, our holdings

07:16.576 --> 07:18.586 align:middle
of securities will continue

07:18.586 --> 07:20.646 align:middle
to support accommodative
financial conditions.

07:21.176 --> 07:25.236 align:middle
Our decision today to begin
tapering our asset purchases

07:25.346 --> 07:28.466 align:middle
does not imply any direct
signal regarding our interest

07:28.466 --> 07:29.116 align:middle
rate policy.

07:29.946 --> 07:31.676 align:middle
We continue to articulate
a different

07:31.676 --> 07:34.136 align:middle
and more stringent test
for the economic conditions

07:34.516 --> 07:36.936 align:middle
that would need to be met before
raising the federal funds rate.

07:38.156 --> 07:41.526 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

07:41.616 --> 07:43.826 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

07:44.646 --> 07:47.096 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:47.776 --> 07:50.646 align:middle
We at the Fed will do everything
we can to complete the recovery

07:50.646 --> 07:52.906 align:middle
in employment and achieve
our price-stability goal.

07:53.596 --> 07:54.016 align:middle
Thank you.

07:54.106 --> 07:55.546 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

07:55.606 --> 07:56.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:58.176 --> 07:58.546 align:middle
Thank you.

07:58.546 --> 08:00.156 align:middle
We'll go to Nick at the
Wall Street Journal.

08:00.156 --> 08:03.246 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

08:03.246 --> 08:05.246 align:middle
Hi. Nick Timiraos of
the Wall Street Journal.

08:05.696 --> 08:09.006 align:middle
Chair Powell, the markets
anticipate you will raise rates

08:09.406 --> 08:10.966 align:middle
once or twice next year.

08:11.336 --> 08:12.066 align:middle
Are they wrong?

08:13.396 --> 08:17.106 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:18.306 --> 08:22.146 align:middle
So I would say it this way.

08:22.146 --> 08:27.466 align:middle
We try to focus on what we
can control-and that is how

08:27.466 --> 08:29.816 align:middle
to communicate as
clearly as possible,

08:29.816 --> 08:32.376 align:middle
in this highly uncertain
world, how we're thinking

08:32.376 --> 08:34.516 align:middle
about the economic outlook
and the balance of risks

08:34.866 --> 08:40.876 align:middle
and how policy will evolve in
that case and also in the cases,

08:40.876 --> 08:43.606 align:middle
which are frequent,
where the economy evolves

08:43.606 --> 08:44.596 align:middle
in unexpected ways.

08:45.076 --> 08:46.606 align:middle
So the focus at this meeting is

08:46.606 --> 08:49.366 align:middle
on tapering asset purchases,
not on raising rates.

08:49.826 --> 08:51.616 align:middle
It is time to taper, we think,

08:51.616 --> 08:54.286 align:middle
because the economy has achieved
substantial further progress

08:54.286 --> 08:56.526 align:middle
toward our goals, measured
from last December.

08:56.926 --> 08:59.406 align:middle
We don't think it's time
yet to raise interest rates.

08:59.546 --> 09:03.146 align:middle
There is still ground to cover
to reach maximum employment,

09:03.146 --> 09:06.026 align:middle
both in terms of employment
and in terms of participation.

09:06.936 --> 09:10.646 align:middle
Getting to your question,
our baseline expectation is

09:10.646 --> 09:13.346 align:middle
that supply bottlenecks and
shortages will persist well

09:13.346 --> 09:16.936 align:middle
into next year, and elevated
inflation as well, and that,

09:16.936 --> 09:19.016 align:middle
as the pandemic subsides,

09:19.256 --> 09:22.176 align:middle
supply chain bottlenecks will
abate-and job growth will move

09:22.176 --> 09:22.636 align:middle
back up.

09:23.056 --> 09:25.086 align:middle
And as that happens,
inflation will decline

09:25.406 --> 09:27.066 align:middle
from today's elevated levels.

09:27.066 --> 09:29.286 align:middle
Of course, the timing of
that is highly uncertain.

09:29.826 --> 09:32.016 align:middle
But, certainly, we should
see inflation moving

09:32.016 --> 09:33.536 align:middle
down by the second
or third quarter.

09:34.366 --> 09:36.116 align:middle
The time for lifting
rates and beginning

09:36.116 --> 09:37.666 align:middle
to remove accommodation
will depend

09:37.666 --> 09:38.756 align:middle
on the path of the economy.

09:39.286 --> 09:40.496 align:middle
We think we can be patient.

09:40.726 --> 09:45.206 align:middle
If a response is called
for, we will not hesitate.

09:45.946 --> 09:48.666 align:middle
So, what I will tell you is,
we will be watching carefully

09:48.666 --> 09:50.486 align:middle
to see whether the
economy evolves in line

09:50.486 --> 09:54.446 align:middle
with our expectations, and
policy will adapt appropriately.

09:55.536 --> 09:57.786 align:middle
And that's what I would say.

09:58.366 --> 09:59.556 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

09:59.556 --> 10:02.566 align:middle
Based on-if I could follow up,
based on your current outlook

10:02.566 --> 10:06.306 align:middle
for the labor market, do you
think it's possible-or likely,

10:06.306 --> 10:09.276 align:middle
even-that maximum
employment could be achieved

10:09.276 --> 10:11.266 align:middle
by the second half of next year?

10:13.076 --> 10:13.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:13.406 --> 10:15.596 align:middle
So if you look at the progress
that we've made over the course

10:15.596 --> 10:19.656 align:middle
of the last year, if that
pace were to continue,

10:19.656 --> 10:21.046 align:middle
then the answer would be: "Yes,

10:21.046 --> 10:22.446 align:middle
I do think that that
is possible."

10:23.066 --> 10:27.116 align:middle
Of course, we measure maximum
employment based on a wide range

10:27.116 --> 10:31.246 align:middle
of figures, but it's certainly
within the realm of possibility.

10:31.246 --> 10:31.926 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

10:33.796 --> 10:34.116 align:middle
Thank you.

10:34.116 --> 10:35.186 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:35.186 --> 10:35.536 align:middle
Thank you.

10:35.536 --> 10:38.746 align:middle
Next, we'll go to Jeanna
at the New York Times.

10:38.746 --> 10:41.096 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

10:41.096 --> 10:42.016 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

10:42.136 --> 10:44.636 align:middle
I was wondering if you could
detail a little bit how you're

10:44.636 --> 10:47.286 align:middle
thinking about wages
at this moment.

10:47.286 --> 10:49.726 align:middle
Obviously, we're seeing strong
wage growth, particularly

10:49.726 --> 10:52.456 align:middle
for people in sort of
lower-income fields.

10:52.806 --> 10:54.726 align:middle
I wonder if you see
that as a positive thing

10:54.726 --> 10:58.186 align:middle
or as a potential start to
a wage-price spiral and sort

10:58.186 --> 11:00.406 align:middle
of how you sort of
delineate those two things.

11:00.406 --> 11:01.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:01.616 --> 11:05.676 align:middle
So wages have been moving
up strongly, very strongly.

11:05.676 --> 11:06.976 align:middle
And, in particular,
I would point

11:06.976 --> 11:09.936 align:middle
to the employment
compensation index reading

11:09.936 --> 11:12.396 align:middle
that we got last Friday.

11:12.596 --> 11:16.306 align:middle
Now, in real terms, they've
been-they had been running a

11:16.306 --> 11:17.546 align:middle
little bit below inflation,

11:17.546 --> 11:19.846 align:middle
so real wages were
not really increasing.

11:19.846 --> 11:22.156 align:middle
I think, with the ECI
reading, it becomes close

11:22.156 --> 11:28.946 align:middle
to maybe not increasing,
but close to back to zero

11:29.266 --> 11:30.506 align:middle
in terms of the real increase.

11:30.976 --> 11:34.426 align:middle
So wages moving up, of
course, is how standard

11:34.426 --> 11:36.176 align:middle
of living increases
over the years

11:36.176 --> 11:37.806 align:middle
for generation upon generation.

11:37.806 --> 11:41.056 align:middle
It's very important, and
it's generally a good thing.

11:41.196 --> 11:45.096 align:middle
You know, the concern is a
somewhat unusual case where,

11:45.096 --> 11:49.146 align:middle
if wages were to be rising
persistently and materially

11:49.146 --> 11:53.276 align:middle
above inflation and
productivity gains,

11:53.956 --> 12:00.236 align:middle
that could put upward pressure
on, or downward pressure

12:00.236 --> 12:02.826 align:middle
on margins and cause
companies to-or employers,

12:02.826 --> 12:06.486 align:middle
really-to raise prices
as a result,

12:06.736 --> 12:09.866 align:middle
and you can see yourself,
find yourself in what we used

12:09.866 --> 12:11.256 align:middle
to call a wage-price spiral.

12:11.746 --> 12:13.066 align:middle
We don't have evidence
of that yet.

12:13.276 --> 12:15.146 align:middle
Productivity has been very high.

12:15.686 --> 12:17.966 align:middle
The ECI reading is
just one reading.

12:18.386 --> 12:19.526 align:middle
Again, if you look back,

12:19.736 --> 12:23.006 align:middle
we-so we'll be watching
this carefully.

12:23.526 --> 12:25.386 align:middle
But I would say that,
at this point,

12:25.386 --> 12:29.156 align:middle
we don't see troubling
increases in wages.

12:29.156 --> 12:31.576 align:middle
And we don't expect
those to emerge,

12:31.576 --> 12:32.696 align:middle
but we'll be watching carefully.

12:33.256 --> 12:37.276 align:middle
JOE PAVEL.

12:37.356 --> 12:39.036 align:middle
Next, we'll go to Steve
Liesman from CNBC.

12:39.036 --> 12:42.216 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

12:42.876 --> 12:44.316 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

12:44.696 --> 12:48.896 align:middle
I wonder if you could
perhaps give us your thinking

12:48.896 --> 12:52.226 align:middle
about the tradeoffs between
inflation and unemployment.

12:52.226 --> 12:53.526 align:middle
You talked about the shortfall

12:53.526 --> 12:56.646 align:middle
of unemployment-reemployment
relative to before the pandemic,

12:56.916 --> 12:59.956 align:middle
and yet you have inflation,
which is affecting everybody.

13:00.316 --> 13:03.676 align:middle
Are we at, or close to,
a point where the risk

13:03.676 --> 13:06.016 align:middle
of inflation is greater
than the benefit

13:06.016 --> 13:09.066 align:middle
that you'd-for recovering
these lost jobs so that now,

13:09.066 --> 13:12.126 align:middle
from a risk-management
standpoint, it makes sense

13:12.126 --> 13:13.866 align:middle
to move more aggressively
on rate hikes?

13:13.866 --> 13:15.656 align:middle
And kind of a related question:

13:15.886 --> 13:19.166 align:middle
The statement today says you'll
keep policy accommodative

13:19.166 --> 13:21.516 align:middle
until you hit that 2
percent inflation target.

13:21.516 --> 13:25.436 align:middle
Our surveys show looking for
5 percent inflation this year,

13:25.566 --> 13:29.636 align:middle
3½ percent next year-it sure
seems like you're on track

13:29.636 --> 13:33.206 align:middle
to modestly or moderately
exceed that 2 percent target.

13:33.206 --> 13:34.966 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

13:35.146 --> 13:38.506 align:middle
Yes. So I'm not sure I totally
got your first question,

13:38.506 --> 13:43.366 align:middle
but I would say-in fact, could
you just quickly, succinctly,

13:43.366 --> 13:44.486 align:middle
say your first question again?

13:44.486 --> 13:45.096 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:45.306 --> 13:47.826 align:middle
Sure. The idea that the
tradeoff between inflation

13:47.826 --> 13:51.586 align:middle
and unemployment, that you
would keep policy accommodative

13:51.716 --> 13:55.116 align:middle
to put this 5 million folks, or
find these 5 million jobs again,

13:55.116 --> 13:57.956 align:middle
at the same time all
Americans will be suffering

13:57.956 --> 14:00.406 align:middle
from higher inflation-is
that tradeoff worth it,

14:00.406 --> 14:03.776 align:middle
or is it better or smarter
to raise rates right now

14:03.776 --> 14:07.036 align:middle
to combat inflation and
perhaps not lean so heavily

14:07.036 --> 14:08.336 align:middle
on the employment
side of the mandate?

14:08.556 --> 14:09.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:09.156 --> 14:11.606 align:middle
Yes, so, you know, this isn't
the traditional Phillips curve

14:11.606 --> 14:13.386 align:middle
situation where there's
a direct tradeoff,

14:13.386 --> 14:14.676 align:middle
where that's really what
we're talking about.

14:14.676 --> 14:17.366 align:middle
The inflation that we're
seeing is really not due

14:17.366 --> 14:19.116 align:middle
to a tight labor market.

14:19.116 --> 14:23.316 align:middle
It's due to bottlenecks,
and it's due to shortages,

14:23.316 --> 14:26.186 align:middle
and it's due to very
strong demand meeting those.

14:26.626 --> 14:31.406 align:middle
So I think it's not
the classical situation

14:31.406 --> 14:33.496 align:middle
where you have that
precise tradeoff.

14:33.556 --> 14:35.976 align:middle
But, you know, in
this situation,

14:36.516 --> 14:42.076 align:middle
we do have a provision in our
Statement on Longer-Run Goals,

14:42.076 --> 14:45.106 align:middle
as you know, that says when
those two things are in tension,

14:45.146 --> 14:47.096 align:middle
what we do is, we take

14:47.096 --> 14:48.986 align:middle
into account the
employment shortfalls

14:48.986 --> 14:51.396 align:middle
and inflation deviations and
the potentially different time

14:51.396 --> 14:54.916 align:middle
horizons over which employment
and inflation are projected

14:54.916 --> 14:57.386 align:middle
to return to levels judged
consistent with the mandate.

14:57.386 --> 15:00.476 align:middle
So we used to call that the
"balanced approach" paragraph.

15:00.476 --> 15:03.196 align:middle
We have to think about the
amount of the deviation,

15:03.196 --> 15:04.876 align:middle
we have to think about
the time it will take,

15:05.176 --> 15:07.106 align:middle
and we have to make
policy in a world

15:07.106 --> 15:08.566 align:middle
where the two goals
are in tension.

15:08.716 --> 15:09.726 align:middle
It's very difficult.

15:10.006 --> 15:12.526 align:middle
But what it really boils
down to is something that's

15:13.066 --> 15:13.686 align:middle
common sense.

15:13.686 --> 15:15.096 align:middle
And that is risk management.

15:15.096 --> 15:17.856 align:middle
We have to be aware of the risks

15:17.996 --> 15:20.936 align:middle
that we're-particularly
now the risk

15:20.936 --> 15:22.656 align:middle
of significantly
higher inflation.

15:22.656 --> 15:27.416 align:middle
We see shortages and
bottlenecks persisting

15:27.416 --> 15:29.046 align:middle
into next year, well
into next year.

15:29.046 --> 15:31.996 align:middle
We see higher inflation
persisting, and we have to be

15:31.996 --> 15:36.676 align:middle
in position to address that risk
should it become really a threat

15:36.806 --> 15:40.356 align:middle
to-should it create a
threat of more persistent,

15:40.356 --> 15:41.186 align:middle
longer-term inflation.

15:41.186 --> 15:43.146 align:middle
And that's what we think
our policy is doing now.

15:43.386 --> 15:47.076 align:middle
It's putting us in a position
to be able to address the range

15:47.076 --> 15:48.066 align:middle
of plausible outcomes.

15:48.066 --> 15:48.746 align:middle
JOE PAVEL.

15:52.406 --> 15:52.756 align:middle
Thank you.

15:52.756 --> 15:55.456 align:middle
Next, we'll go to Colby
Smith at the Financial Times.

15:59.066 --> 15:59.626 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

15:59.626 --> 16:00.036 align:middle
Thank you.

16:00.036 --> 16:02.636 align:middle
Chair Powell, what are
the economic conditions

16:02.806 --> 16:06.666 align:middle
that would perhaps warrant
a faster pace of tapering?

16:06.896 --> 16:09.766 align:middle
And I'm wondering how you would
also characterize the risks

16:09.806 --> 16:11.926 align:middle
that the Fed may actually
need to accelerate

16:11.926 --> 16:13.126 align:middle
that process eventually.

16:13.126 --> 16:13.666 align:middle
Thank you.

16:14.186 --> 16:14.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:14.976 --> 16:17.876 align:middle
So I guess, as I said
in my opening remarks,

16:17.876 --> 16:21.646 align:middle
assuming the economy
performs broadly as expected,

16:22.266 --> 16:25.266 align:middle
the Committee judges that
similar reductions in the pace

16:25.266 --> 16:28.036 align:middle
of net asset purchases will
likely be appropriate each

16:28.036 --> 16:31.856 align:middle
month, and we're prepared
to deviate from that path

16:31.856 --> 16:34.166 align:middle
if warranted by changes
in the economic outlook.

16:34.166 --> 16:38.486 align:middle
So I'm not going to give
you a lot more detail

16:38.486 --> 16:39.536 align:middle
on what that might be.

16:39.536 --> 16:42.196 align:middle
Of course, if we do see
something like that happening,

16:42.196 --> 16:43.216 align:middle
if it becomes a question,

16:43.216 --> 16:45.196 align:middle
then we'll communicate
very transparently

16:45.196 --> 16:46.856 align:middle
and openly about that.

16:46.856 --> 16:48.656 align:middle
But I'm just going to
leave it with the words

16:48.656 --> 16:49.516 align:middle
that are in the statement.

16:49.716 --> 16:50.736 align:middle
Sorry, was there a second part?

16:51.536 --> 16:51.936 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

16:51.936 --> 16:54.126 align:middle
Yes. It's just on
characterizing the risks

16:54.126 --> 16:57.076 align:middle
that you might actually
have to do so later on.

16:57.946 --> 16:58.176 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:58.316 --> 17:00.956 align:middle
You know, I would just leave
you with the words we have here.

17:01.196 --> 17:07.856 align:middle
We are prepared to speed up or
slow down the pace of reductions

17:07.856 --> 17:10.626 align:middle
in asset purchases if
it's warranted by changes

17:10.626 --> 17:11.616 align:middle
in the economic outlook.

17:11.946 --> 17:14.736 align:middle
And, again, if we feel like
something like that's happening,

17:14.736 --> 17:16.696 align:middle
then we'll be very
transparent about it.

17:16.696 --> 17:18.446 align:middle
We wouldn't want to
surprise markets.

17:18.896 --> 17:22.906 align:middle
We'll say, in light of this
factor or these factors,

17:23.316 --> 17:26.016 align:middle
we are considering doing this,

17:26.016 --> 17:27.556 align:middle
and then we would either
do it or not do it.

17:27.556 --> 17:30.336 align:middle
But so-but I'm not going to, you
know, start making up examples

17:30.416 --> 17:31.316 align:middle
of what that might be today.

17:31.316 --> 17:31.776 align:middle
Thanks. COLBY SMITH.

17:31.776 --> 17:32.196 align:middle
Thank you.

17:32.196 --> 17:32.406 align:middle
JOE PAVEL.

17:33.056 --> 17:33.496 align:middle
Thank you.

17:39.706 --> 17:42.486 align:middle
Next one, Rachel Siegel
at the Washington Post.

17:42.516 --> 17:44.146 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

17:44.146 --> 17:44.656 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

17:44.656 --> 17:47.136 align:middle
Thank you so much for
taking our questions.

17:47.426 --> 17:48.436 align:middle
You mentioned at the beginning

17:48.436 --> 17:50.786 align:middle
that the Fed understands
the difficulties

17:50.786 --> 17:53.856 align:middle
that high inflation poses
for individuals and families,

17:53.856 --> 17:55.726 align:middle
especially those
with limited means.

17:55.906 --> 17:58.526 align:middle
What is your message to
those families or consumers

17:58.526 --> 18:00.696 align:middle
that are struggling with
higher prices right now,

18:00.696 --> 18:03.066 align:middle
and do you feel that
your expectations

18:03.066 --> 18:07.016 align:middle
around transitory inflation,
that message is reaching them?

18:07.086 --> 18:07.546 align:middle
Thank you.

18:08.756 --> 18:09.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:09.976 --> 18:16.576 align:middle
Yes. So, first of all, it
is our job to-and we accept

18:16.576 --> 18:18.136 align:middle
responsibility and
accountability

18:18.636 --> 18:21.866 align:middle
for inflation in
the medium term.

18:21.866 --> 18:24.366 align:middle
We're accountable to Congress
and to the American people

18:24.366 --> 18:26.856 align:middle
for maximum employment
and price stability.

18:27.296 --> 18:29.636 align:middle
The level of inflation
we have right now is not

18:29.636 --> 18:31.136 align:middle
at all consistent
with price stability.

18:31.486 --> 18:32.406 align:middle
By the way, we're also not

18:32.406 --> 18:34.076 align:middle
at maximum employment,
as I mentioned.

18:34.076 --> 18:38.626 align:middle
So I would want to assure people
that we will use our tools

18:38.626 --> 18:41.476 align:middle
as appropriate to get
inflation under control.

18:42.256 --> 18:44.526 align:middle
We don't think it's a good time
to raise interest rates, though,

18:44.526 --> 18:46.666 align:middle
because we want to see the
labor market heal further.

18:46.846 --> 18:49.586 align:middle
And we have very good reason
to think that that will happen

18:49.586 --> 18:57.886 align:middle
as the Delta variant declines,
so-which it's doing now.

18:57.886 --> 19:01.396 align:middle
So "transitory" is a word

19:01.396 --> 19:06.046 align:middle
that people have had
different understandings of.

19:06.046 --> 19:10.806 align:middle
For some, it carries a sense
of "short lived," and that's,

19:10.806 --> 19:12.706 align:middle
you know, there's a real
time component-measured

19:12.706 --> 19:13.966 align:middle
in months, let's say.

19:14.386 --> 19:16.746 align:middle
Really, for us, what
"transitory" has meant is

19:16.746 --> 19:18.716 align:middle
that if something is transitory,

19:18.716 --> 19:21.186 align:middle
it will not leave
behind it permanently

19:21.186 --> 19:24.066 align:middle
or very persistently
higher inflation.

19:24.426 --> 19:25.656 align:middle
So that's why we, you know,

19:25.656 --> 19:27.486 align:middle
we took a step back
from "transitory."

19:27.486 --> 19:30.186 align:middle
We said "expected to be
transitory," first of all,

19:30.186 --> 19:33.476 align:middle
to show uncertainty around
that-we've always said that,

19:33.476 --> 19:35.356 align:middle
by the way, in other contexts;
we just hadn't done it

19:35.356 --> 19:38.296 align:middle
in the statement-but also
to acknowledge, really,

19:38.296 --> 19:41.466 align:middle
that it means different
things to different people.

19:41.466 --> 19:42.966 align:middle
And then we added some language

19:43.046 --> 19:47.196 align:middle
to really explain more
what we're talking

19:47.196 --> 19:49.766 align:middle
about in paragraph two
and paragraph three.

19:49.766 --> 19:52.606 align:middle
We said that "supply and
demand imbalances related

19:52.606 --> 19:53.796 align:middle
to the pandemic and
the reopening

19:53.796 --> 19:56.916 align:middle
of the economy have contributed
to sizable price increases,"

19:57.386 --> 19:59.766 align:middle
and we said, "Progress on
vaccinations and an easing

19:59.766 --> 20:01.556 align:middle
of supply constraints
are expected

20:01.556 --> 20:04.596 align:middle
to support continued gains in
economic activity and employment

20:04.596 --> 20:06.576 align:middle
as well as a reduction
in inflation."

20:06.576 --> 20:09.046 align:middle
So we're trying to
explain what we mean

20:09.636 --> 20:12.636 align:middle
and also acknowledging more
uncertainty about "transitory."

20:12.636 --> 20:15.856 align:middle
So it's, I mean, it's become
a word that's attracted a lot

20:15.856 --> 20:18.986 align:middle
of attention that maybe is
distracting from our message,

20:18.986 --> 20:21.116 align:middle
which we want to be
as clear as possible.

20:21.756 --> 20:24.546 align:middle
Ultimately, the only other
thing I would say is, look,

20:24.546 --> 20:28.206 align:middle
we understand completely
that it's particularly people

20:28.206 --> 20:30.076 align:middle
who are living paycheck
to paycheck

20:30.076 --> 20:31.526 align:middle
or seeing higher grocery costs,

20:31.526 --> 20:34.026 align:middle
higher gasoline costs-when
the winter comes,

20:34.026 --> 20:35.836 align:middle
higher heating costs
for their homes.

20:36.116 --> 20:38.596 align:middle
We understand completely
what they're going through.

20:39.506 --> 20:43.116 align:middle
And, you know, we will use our
tools over time to make sure

20:43.116 --> 20:46.726 align:middle
that that doesn't become a
permanent feature of life.

20:46.846 --> 20:49.426 align:middle
Really, that's one of
our principal jobs,

20:49.426 --> 20:51.196 align:middle
along with achieving
maximum employment.

20:51.856 --> 20:52.676 align:middle
And that's our commitment.

20:52.676 --> 20:53.346 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:55.136 --> 20:55.566 align:middle
Thank you.

20:55.566 --> 20:58.216 align:middle
We'll go to Chris Rugaber
of the Associated Press.

20:59.906 --> 21:00.316 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

21:00.316 --> 21:01.146 align:middle
Great. Thank you, Michelle.

21:01.326 --> 21:02.566 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

21:02.566 --> 21:05.116 align:middle
Well, I wonder if
you could update us.

21:05.116 --> 21:08.156 align:middle
You talked about getting
back to full employment,

21:08.156 --> 21:14.256 align:middle
and so could you update
how you define that?

21:14.256 --> 21:17.146 align:middle
I mean, you've, you know-a
few months ago, yourself

21:17.146 --> 21:19.716 align:middle
and other Fed officials
talked about getting back

21:20.066 --> 21:21.696 align:middle
to the pre-COVID labor market.

21:22.216 --> 21:23.666 align:middle
There were even hints
you might try

21:23.666 --> 21:25.816 align:middle
to do something better
than that.

21:26.006 --> 21:29.006 align:middle
Now we heard talk of, as you
mentioned, people retiring,

21:29.006 --> 21:31.276 align:middle
and there's talk
of not being able

21:31.746 --> 21:34.006 align:middle
to get back as-all the
jobs back because of

21:34.006 --> 21:35.226 align:middle
that and other trends.

21:36.216 --> 21:38.106 align:middle
You did mention the
prime-age folks.

21:38.356 --> 21:42.716 align:middle
So can you give us some examples
of things you're looking at,

21:42.946 --> 21:44.796 align:middle
specifically, to
measure full employment?

21:44.796 --> 21:47.806 align:middle
Will you be looking at prime-age
employment population ratio,

21:47.806 --> 21:51.156 align:middle
for example, and, if so, do
you need to see it get back

21:51.366 --> 21:56.256 align:middle
to pre-COVID levels to
achieve maximum employment,

21:56.256 --> 21:58.706 align:middle
or is there something short
of that that would work?

21:59.266 --> 21:59.586 align:middle
Thank you.

21:59.586 --> 21:59.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:00.156 --> 22:02.196 align:middle
Thanks. So maximum
employment is,

22:03.136 --> 22:04.876 align:middle
it's a broad-what would
we say?-broad-based

22:04.876 --> 22:07.916 align:middle
and inclusive goal that's not
directly measurable and changes

22:07.916 --> 22:10.656 align:middle
over time due to
various factors.

22:11.226 --> 22:12.626 align:middle
You can't specify
a specific goal.

22:12.626 --> 22:16.186 align:middle
So it's taking into account
quite a broad range of things,

22:16.336 --> 22:19.006 align:middle
and, of course,
employment-levels

22:19.006 --> 22:21.836 align:middle
of employment-[and]
participation are a part

22:21.836 --> 22:22.336 align:middle
of that.

22:22.336 --> 22:25.706 align:middle
But, in addition, there are
other measures of what's going

22:25.706 --> 22:28.576 align:middle
on in the labor market,
like wages is a key measure

22:28.576 --> 22:32.406 align:middle
of how tight the labor
market is-the level of quits,

22:32.856 --> 22:35.666 align:middle
the amount of job
openings, the flows in

22:35.666 --> 22:36.926 align:middle
and out of various states.

22:36.926 --> 22:38.896 align:middle
So we look at so many
different things,

22:39.366 --> 22:40.826 align:middle
and you make an overall
judgment.

22:41.006 --> 22:44.906 align:middle
Now, the temptation at the
beginning of the recovery was

22:44.906 --> 22:48.176 align:middle
to look at the data in
February of 2020 and say, well,

22:48.176 --> 22:51.026 align:middle
that's the goal because we
didn't know any-that's what

22:51.026 --> 22:51.496 align:middle
we knew.

22:51.636 --> 22:54.226 align:middle
We knew that was achievable
in a context of low inflation.

22:54.726 --> 22:56.786 align:middle
I think we're in a, you
know, we're learning that.

22:56.786 --> 23:00.026 align:middle
We have to be humble about what
we know about this economy,

23:00.746 --> 23:04.906 align:middle
which is still very, you know,
COVID-affected, by the way.

23:04.906 --> 23:05.926 align:middle
A lot of what we're seeing

23:05.926 --> 23:08.026 align:middle
in the last 90 days
is because of Delta.

23:08.026 --> 23:09.836 align:middle
We were on a path to a
very different place.

23:10.416 --> 23:12.756 align:middle
Delta put us on a
different path.

23:12.756 --> 23:14.096 align:middle
And we see these things.

23:14.136 --> 23:17.496 align:middle
But, so I think we're going to
have to-ideally, we would have,

23:17.946 --> 23:21.776 align:middle
we would see further development
of the labor market in a context

23:21.776 --> 23:25.396 align:middle
where there isn't another COVID
spike, and then we would be able

23:25.396 --> 23:26.496 align:middle
to see, I think, a lot.

23:26.496 --> 23:29.576 align:middle
We would see whether-how
does participation react

23:29.576 --> 23:32.146 align:middle
in that world, in that
sort of post-COVID world?

23:32.816 --> 23:35.506 align:middle
Right now, people are staying
out of the labor market

23:35.506 --> 23:38.056 align:middle
because of caretaking [and]
because of fear of COVID

23:38.666 --> 23:40.536 align:middle
to a significant extent.

23:41.146 --> 23:44.406 align:middle
You know, we thought
that schools reopening

23:44.406 --> 23:48.376 align:middle
and elapsing unemployment
benefits would produce some sort

23:48.376 --> 23:50.776 align:middle
of additional labor supply.

23:50.776 --> 23:53.046 align:middle
That doesn't seem to have
been the case, interestingly.

23:53.046 --> 23:56.506 align:middle
So I think there's room for
a whole lot of humility here,

23:57.096 --> 24:00.086 align:middle
as we try to think about what
maximum employment would be.

24:00.086 --> 24:03.306 align:middle
We're going to have to
see some time post-COVID

24:03.306 --> 24:07.026 align:middle
so that we know-or post-Delta,
anyway-to see what is possible.

24:07.446 --> 24:10.046 align:middle
And I think the learning
for those of us who lived

24:10.046 --> 24:14.656 align:middle
through the last cycle is that,
over time, you can get to places

24:14.686 --> 24:16.326 align:middle
that didn't look possible.

24:16.326 --> 24:19.346 align:middle
Now, what we also
have now, though, is,

24:19.346 --> 24:20.226 align:middle
we have high inflation.

24:20.266 --> 24:22.056 align:middle
So we have a completely
different situation now

24:22.056 --> 24:24.536 align:middle
where we have high inflation,
and we have to balance

24:24.536 --> 24:27.686 align:middle
that with what's going on
in the employment market.

24:27.686 --> 24:30.966 align:middle
So it's a complicated situation,
and I would say, we will,

24:31.416 --> 24:34.346 align:middle
we hope to achieve
significantly greater clarity

24:34.706 --> 24:36.936 align:middle
about where this
economy is going

24:36.936 --> 24:40.186 align:middle
and what the characteristics of
the post-pandemic economy are

24:40.686 --> 24:42.026 align:middle
over the first half
of next year.

24:43.216 --> 24:43.546 align:middle
JOE PAVEL.

24:46.296 --> 24:48.406 align:middle
Thanks. We'll go to Howard
Schneider at Reuters.

24:48.496 --> 24:49.956 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

24:49.956 --> 24:51.066 align:middle
Thanks, and thanks
for doing this.

24:51.066 --> 24:53.366 align:middle
So, given that answer
about employment,

24:53.366 --> 24:56.816 align:middle
I would like to get back to
Steve's question a little bit.

24:56.816 --> 24:59.626 align:middle
On a headline basis, just
as it's evolved this year,

24:59.626 --> 25:02.486 align:middle
do you feel that the two tests
on inflation have been met?

25:02.956 --> 25:04.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:04.066 --> 25:05.576 align:middle
Sorry, the two tests?

25:05.576 --> 25:05.856 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

25:06.206 --> 25:08.226 align:middle
The two tests in the
statement-the guidance

25:08.226 --> 25:10.136 align:middle
that it has to hit 2
percent and be on track

25:10.136 --> 25:12.066 align:middle
to moderately be
above 2 percent.

25:12.066 --> 25:13.376 align:middle
Has the economy cleared that?

25:13.896 --> 25:14.106 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:14.166 --> 25:15.646 align:middle
That's a decision
for the Committee.

25:15.646 --> 25:17.216 align:middle
I would put it to you this way.

25:17.726 --> 25:20.186 align:middle
When we reach maximum
employment,

25:20.186 --> 25:23.976 align:middle
when we reach a state where
labor market conditions are

25:23.976 --> 25:25.976 align:middle
at maximum employment in
the Committee's judgment,

25:26.346 --> 25:29.746 align:middle
it's very possible that the
inflation test will already

25:29.746 --> 25:30.126 align:middle
be met.

25:31.126 --> 25:33.876 align:middle
We're aware that that language
sounds, it sounds a little

25:33.876 --> 25:36.566 align:middle
out of touch with what's
going on, but, you know,

25:36.566 --> 25:38.116 align:middle
we're not at maximum employment.

25:38.436 --> 25:40.906 align:middle
When that is the case,

25:41.516 --> 25:44.106 align:middle
we'll look to see whether
the inflation test is met,

25:44.726 --> 25:46.886 align:middle
and there's a good chance
that it will be if you look

25:46.886 --> 25:49.306 align:middle
at how inflation has evolved
in the last year and a half.

25:49.686 --> 25:50.566 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

25:50.726 --> 25:53.206 align:middle
So, to follow up, so
you're not willing to commit

25:53.736 --> 25:56.196 align:middle
that the current
levels of inflation

25:56.196 --> 25:57.806 align:middle
and their persistence have met

25:58.336 --> 26:00.036 align:middle
"moderately" and
"for some time."

26:00.556 --> 26:04.726 align:middle
So, given that, I mean,
how should we wonder what

26:04.726 --> 26:08.266 align:middle
"moderately" over and
"for some time" mean?

26:08.376 --> 26:09.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:09.126 --> 26:10.086 align:middle
What I'm really saying is,

26:10.086 --> 26:13.256 align:middle
that question is not
before us right now.

26:13.256 --> 26:15.066 align:middle
You know, we had the
question on when to taper.

26:15.066 --> 26:16.356 align:middle
We've now answered that question

26:16.356 --> 26:18.146 align:middle
and the speed of
it and all that.

26:18.146 --> 26:20.576 align:middle
We have not focused on whether
we meet the liftoff test,

26:20.576 --> 26:22.106 align:middle
because we don't meet
the liftoff test now

26:22.106 --> 26:23.666 align:middle
because we're not at
maximum employment.

26:23.966 --> 26:26.536 align:middle
What I'm saying is,
given where inflation is

26:26.536 --> 26:27.906 align:middle
and where it's projected to be,

26:28.776 --> 26:30.906 align:middle
let's say we do meet the
maximum-employment test.

26:30.906 --> 26:32.496 align:middle
Then the Committee-the
question for the Committee

26:32.496 --> 26:36.646 align:middle
at that time will be, has
the inflation test been met?

26:36.646 --> 26:39.076 align:middle
And, you know, I don't want
to get ahead of the Committee

26:39.076 --> 26:42.176 align:middle
on that, but the answer may very
well be "Yes, it's been met."

26:42.646 --> 26:44.096 align:middle
But we're not asking
that question today,

26:44.096 --> 26:46.346 align:middle
because we're not
running a liftoff test.

26:46.676 --> 26:48.306 align:middle
We're not evaluating
the liftoff test today.

26:48.306 --> 26:51.006 align:middle
We didn't have that
discussion at today's meeting.

26:51.006 --> 26:53.166 align:middle
We did talk about the
economy and the development

26:53.166 --> 26:55.256 align:middle
of the economy, but we didn't
ask ourselves whether the

26:55.256 --> 26:58.846 align:middle
liftoff test is met, because,
you know, it's clearly not met

26:58.846 --> 27:00.126 align:middle
on the maximum-employment side.

27:00.616 --> 27:01.906 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

27:01.906 --> 27:02.236 align:middle
Thank you.

27:02.236 --> 27:03.336 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:03.336 --> 27:03.836 align:middle
Thank you.

27:03.966 --> 27:05.956 align:middle
Let's go to Matthew
Boesler at Bloomberg.

27:05.956 --> 27:08.496 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

27:08.496 --> 27:09.176 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

27:09.176 --> 27:11.006 align:middle
Matthew Boesler with Bloomberg.

27:11.006 --> 27:14.496 align:middle
So, when you're looking at this
question of assessing whether

27:14.496 --> 27:17.586 align:middle
or not the U.S. economy
is at maximum employment,

27:17.746 --> 27:20.236 align:middle
do you have a framework
for making that judgment

27:20.236 --> 27:23.776 align:middle
that is independent of
what inflation is doing?

27:24.106 --> 27:28.746 align:middle
And, if not, does it kind of
complicate that assessment,

27:28.986 --> 27:31.766 align:middle
given all of the uncertainty
about inflation right now

27:31.936 --> 27:35.656 align:middle
and the inclination to
believe that, you know,

27:35.656 --> 27:37.746 align:middle
the high inflation we're
seeing is not related

27:37.746 --> 27:41.676 align:middle
to capacity utilization
in the labor market?

27:41.676 --> 27:42.116 align:middle
Thank you.

27:42.866 --> 27:43.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:43.906 --> 27:50.906 align:middle
So we don't actually define
maximum employment as-we define

27:50.906 --> 27:53.026 align:middle
it in terms of inflation,
but, of course,

27:53.026 --> 27:53.986 align:middle
there is a connection there.

27:53.986 --> 27:55.626 align:middle
Maximum employment
has to be a level

27:55.626 --> 27:58.296 align:middle
that is consistent
with stable prices.

27:59.026 --> 28:01.156 align:middle
But that's not really
how we think about it.

28:01.156 --> 28:04.726 align:middle
We think about maximum
employment as looking

28:04.726 --> 28:05.996 align:middle
at a broad range of things.

28:05.996 --> 28:08.056 align:middle
You can't just look
at-unlike inflation,

28:08.056 --> 28:09.306 align:middle
where you can have a number.

28:09.836 --> 28:13.366 align:middle
But with maximum employment,
you could be in a situation,

28:13.366 --> 28:17.836 align:middle
hypothetically, where the
unemployment rate is low,

28:17.836 --> 28:20.106 align:middle
but there are many people who
are out of the labor force

28:20.406 --> 28:21.506 align:middle
and will come back in.

28:21.636 --> 28:23.876 align:middle
And so you wouldn't really
be at maximum employment,

28:23.876 --> 28:26.666 align:middle
because there's this group that
isn't counted as unemployed.

28:27.376 --> 28:29.776 align:middle
So we look at a range of things.

28:30.386 --> 28:34.246 align:middle
So the thing is,
by many measures,

28:34.246 --> 28:36.586 align:middle
we are at a very
tight labor market.

28:36.586 --> 28:40.756 align:middle
I mentioned quits and job
openings and wages and things.

28:40.756 --> 28:42.736 align:middle
Many of them are signaling
a tight labor market,

28:42.736 --> 28:46.616 align:middle
but the issue is, how
persistent is that?

28:46.616 --> 28:49.236 align:middle
Because you have people who are
held out of the labor market,

28:49.696 --> 28:52.516 align:middle
you know, of their
own-they're holding themselves

28:52.516 --> 28:56.896 align:middle
out of the labor market because
of caretaking needs or because

28:56.896 --> 28:58.356 align:middle
of fear of COVID, or,

28:58.356 --> 29:00.166 align:middle
for whatever reason,
they're staying out.

29:00.506 --> 29:03.506 align:middle
And it's clear that there are,
you know-with tremendous demand

29:03.506 --> 29:06.986 align:middle
for workers and wages moving
up, it does seem like we're set

29:06.986 --> 29:09.176 align:middle
up to go back to a
higher job-creation rate.

29:09.176 --> 29:10.566 align:middle
So that would suggest
that you're not

29:10.566 --> 29:11.896 align:middle
at maximum employment.

29:11.896 --> 29:14.296 align:middle
So, at the end of the day,
it is a judgment thing.

29:14.656 --> 29:17.426 align:middle
But, of course, at the end of
it, it also has to be a level

29:17.426 --> 29:19.816 align:middle
of employment that's
consistent with price stability.

29:19.816 --> 29:20.366 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

29:21.436 --> 29:23.246 align:middle
And if I could just follow
up briefly, you know,

29:23.246 --> 29:25.396 align:middle
you've talked a little
bit about this possibility

29:25.396 --> 29:28.716 align:middle
that the two goals might be in
tension and how you would have

29:28.716 --> 29:30.556 align:middle
to balance those two things.

29:30.636 --> 29:33.736 align:middle
Could you talk a little bit
about what the Fed's process

29:34.396 --> 29:38.306 align:middle
for balancing those two goals
would be in the event that, say,

29:38.306 --> 29:40.206 align:middle
come next year, you decide
there's a serious risk

29:40.206 --> 29:43.256 align:middle
of persistent inflationary
pressures despite ongoing

29:43.256 --> 29:44.306 align:middle
employment shortfalls?

29:45.136 --> 29:45.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:45.616 --> 29:48.276 align:middle
Yes. I mean, again, it's
a risk-management thing.

29:48.276 --> 29:52.086 align:middle
It's not-I can't reduce it to
an equation, but, ultimately,

29:52.136 --> 29:53.546 align:middle
it's about risk management.

29:53.546 --> 30:01.806 align:middle
So you want to be in a position
to act, to cover the full range

30:01.806 --> 30:03.916 align:middle
of plausible outcomes,
not just the base case.

30:03.916 --> 30:06.306 align:middle
And in this case,
the risk is skewed.

30:07.026 --> 30:10.026 align:middle
For now, it appears to be
skewed toward higher inflation.

30:10.476 --> 30:12.206 align:middle
So we need to be in
a position to act

30:12.296 --> 30:16.366 align:middle
in case it becomes necessary to
do so or appropriate to do so,

30:16.646 --> 30:17.546 align:middle
and we think we will be.

30:18.306 --> 30:21.906 align:middle
So that's how we're
thinking about it.

30:21.906 --> 30:25.076 align:middle
And I think, though,
that, judgmentally, too,

30:25.076 --> 30:26.806 align:middle
it's appropriate to be patient.

30:26.806 --> 30:30.906 align:middle
It's appropriate for us to
see what the labor market

30:30.906 --> 30:33.306 align:middle
and what the economy look
like when they heal further.

30:33.306 --> 30:35.836 align:middle
We know that we were on a
path to a different place,

30:35.836 --> 30:38.126 align:middle
as I mentioned, when
Delta arrived.

30:38.616 --> 30:41.266 align:middle
And Delta stopped, it
stopped job creation.

30:41.666 --> 30:46.636 align:middle
It stopped that transition away
from a goods-focused economy

30:46.636 --> 30:48.336 align:middle
where there's excess
demand for goods

30:48.336 --> 30:50.666 align:middle
because there-services
are not available.

30:50.666 --> 30:51.566 align:middle
People are not traveling.

30:51.956 --> 30:55.416 align:middle
That transition itself could
help bring inflation down,

30:55.416 --> 30:58.056 align:middle
because, presumably, people
would spend a little less

30:58.056 --> 31:01.136 align:middle
on goods while they start
spending more on travel

31:01.376 --> 31:03.846 align:middle
and all sorts of travel
services and things like that.

31:03.846 --> 31:08.686 align:middle
So we want to see that
healthy process unfold

31:08.686 --> 31:11.976 align:middle
as we decide what the true
state of the economy is,

31:11.976 --> 31:13.506 align:middle
and we think it will
evolve in a way

31:13.816 --> 31:15.896 align:middle
that will mean lower inflation.

31:16.386 --> 31:17.946 align:middle
Bottlenecks should be abating.

31:17.946 --> 31:20.866 align:middle
We start to see that now with
some of them, but, overall,

31:20.866 --> 31:22.586 align:middle
they haven't gotten better
overall, and, you know,

31:22.586 --> 31:23.556 align:middle
we're very aware of that.

31:24.136 --> 31:26.806 align:middle
So that's really how
we're thinking about it.

31:26.806 --> 31:28.656 align:middle
We're thinking that
time will tell us more.

31:28.796 --> 31:31.316 align:middle
In the meantime, we don't think
it's time to raise rates now.

31:31.866 --> 31:36.326 align:middle
You know, if we do conclude
that it's necessary to do so,

31:36.326 --> 31:39.706 align:middle
then we'll be patient,
but we won't hesitate.

31:39.706 --> 31:40.346 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:42.466 --> 31:42.826 align:middle
Thank you.

31:42.826 --> 31:45.176 align:middle
Let's go to Edward
Lawrence at Fox Business.

31:45.176 --> 31:46.126 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

31:46.126 --> 31:47.816 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman,
for taking the call.

31:47.816 --> 31:49.796 align:middle
So the Federal Reserve-I want
to talk about climate change.

31:49.796 --> 31:51.816 align:middle
The Federal Reserve released
a statement today-says

31:51.856 --> 31:53.916 align:middle
"the Federal Reserve
supports the efforts .

31:53.916 --> 31:56.736 align:middle
. . to identify key issues
and potential solutions

31:56.736 --> 31:59.416 align:middle
for the climate-related
challenges most relevant

31:59.416 --> 32:01.566 align:middle
to central banks and
supervisory" outcomes.

32:01.946 --> 32:03.196 align:middle
Is this putting us on the path

32:03.506 --> 32:06.836 align:middle
to regulate what banks can
offer loans on or invest in,

32:07.086 --> 32:08.616 align:middle
like coal plants
or fossil fuels?

32:09.566 --> 32:09.926 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:09.926 --> 32:11.106 align:middle
So that's not a decision

32:11.106 --> 32:14.306 align:middle
for bank regulators
or for any agency.

32:14.306 --> 32:16.506 align:middle
That's a decision for
elected representatives.

32:16.776 --> 32:22.226 align:middle
So we feel that any role that we
have-and we do think we have a

32:22.226 --> 32:23.536 align:middle
role in climate change.

32:23.536 --> 32:25.036 align:middle
It relates to our
existing mandates,

32:25.036 --> 32:28.866 align:middle
and our existing mandates are,
really, prudential regulation

32:28.866 --> 32:30.216 align:middle
of financial institutions.

32:30.676 --> 32:33.906 align:middle
We expect them, and the public
will expect us to expect them,

32:33.906 --> 32:36.506 align:middle
to understand and be in a
position to manage their risks.

32:36.936 --> 32:38.136 align:middle
So that's physical risk,

32:38.136 --> 32:39.676 align:middle
and it's transition
risk for climate.

32:39.906 --> 32:42.376 align:middle
And, by the way, the large
financial institutions are doing

32:42.376 --> 32:45.416 align:middle
this already, and, you know,

32:45.516 --> 32:47.086 align:middle
we think that's right
within our mandate.

32:47.216 --> 32:49.366 align:middle
There's also a financial
stability question,

32:49.896 --> 32:52.276 align:middle
the overall stability
of the financial system.

32:52.746 --> 32:55.596 align:middle
And so, from that
standpoint, we can do research.

32:55.596 --> 32:58.246 align:middle
We can try to help understand
what will the pathways be

32:58.566 --> 33:01.486 align:middle
through which climate change
effects the economy-both

33:01.486 --> 33:03.136 align:middle
physical risk and
transition risk.

33:03.136 --> 33:06.356 align:middle
That's what we can do, and
that's what we will do.

33:06.356 --> 33:07.356 align:middle
And we'll do it well.

33:07.356 --> 33:10.746 align:middle
Within the frame of our existing
mandates, we'll do it well.

33:10.786 --> 33:14.716 align:middle
We're not the people who will
decide the national strategy

33:15.176 --> 33:16.556 align:middle
on climate change.

33:16.556 --> 33:21.366 align:middle
That has to be elected
people, and not so much us.

33:21.366 --> 33:25.066 align:middle
We feel like we have that narrow
mandate, and we will do it well.

33:25.536 --> 33:26.726 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

33:26.836 --> 33:27.126 align:middle
Thank you.

33:27.126 --> 33:27.616 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:27.616 --> 33:28.376 align:middle
Great, thank you.

33:28.376 --> 33:30.046 align:middle
Victoria Guida at Politico.

33:31.676 --> 33:32.886 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

33:32.886 --> 33:33.486 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

33:33.486 --> 33:35.726 align:middle
So the Fed recently
announced that there is going

33:35.726 --> 33:38.676 align:middle
to be new conflict-of-interest
rules for investments

33:38.676 --> 33:42.016 align:middle
by Fed officials, and
this follows, obviously,

33:42.016 --> 33:44.746 align:middle
the resignation of two
regional Fed presidents.

33:44.746 --> 33:47.606 align:middle
And I'm just wondering, do
you think that there is more

33:47.606 --> 33:50.646 align:middle
that you will need to do
to rebuild the credibility

33:50.646 --> 33:53.506 align:middle
of the Fed, such as, you
know, requiring officials

33:53.506 --> 33:55.766 align:middle
to put their assets
in blind trusts?

33:55.766 --> 33:59.636 align:middle
And also, if you could speak to
whether you have any concerns

33:59.636 --> 34:02.846 align:middle
that any rules or laws were
broken by Fed officials.

34:02.846 --> 34:05.146 align:middle
Thank you.

34:05.376 --> 34:05.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:05.816 --> 34:11.086 align:middle
So we, you know, we-let me
just say that this system,

34:11.086 --> 34:13.526 align:middle
the ethics system we had
in place had been in place

34:13.526 --> 34:18.056 align:middle
for decades and had, as far
as we know, served us well,

34:18.806 --> 34:20.346 align:middle
and then that was
no longer the case.

34:20.456 --> 34:24.706 align:middle
And so we had no moment
of denial about that.

34:24.706 --> 34:26.366 align:middle
As a group, we stepped in,

34:26.366 --> 34:28.036 align:middle
and we took the actions
that we took.

34:28.866 --> 34:33.666 align:middle
And, you know, within one FOMC
cycle, we announced a new set

34:33.666 --> 34:40.826 align:middle
of rules to, you know, to try to
put us back where we need to be,

34:40.826 --> 34:42.876 align:middle
which is, we need to
have the complete trust

34:42.876 --> 34:44.386 align:middle
of the American people
that we're working

34:44.386 --> 34:46.576 align:middle
in their interest all the
time-absolutely critical

34:46.576 --> 34:49.606 align:middle
to our work, as it is for
any government agency.

34:50.116 --> 34:52.476 align:middle
And I feel like this
called that into question.

34:52.476 --> 34:55.436 align:middle
So we reacted, I
would characterize it,

34:55.436 --> 34:57.776 align:middle
strongly and forcefully.

34:58.306 --> 35:00.536 align:middle
If there were other
things that we could do

35:00.536 --> 35:02.816 align:middle
that were reasonable, we
would certainly do them.

35:03.206 --> 35:04.676 align:middle
So you asked me about
blind trusts.

35:04.676 --> 35:09.956 align:middle
You know, the overall authority
for ethics around these issues

35:10.106 --> 35:11.486 align:middle
in the federal government
is the Office

35:11.486 --> 35:12.776 align:middle
of Government Ethics, OGE.

35:12.776 --> 35:16.636 align:middle
And they have a long-held
position

35:18.156 --> 35:19.886 align:middle
which is not favorable
to blind trusts.

35:19.886 --> 35:21.066 align:middle
They do not encourage them.

35:21.066 --> 35:22.376 align:middle
They don't think
they're effective.

35:22.376 --> 35:24.236 align:middle
They think they're cumbersome.

35:24.336 --> 35:26.866 align:middle
And they think there
are better ways to get

35:26.866 --> 35:28.936 align:middle
at the things that
need to be done.

35:28.936 --> 35:30.906 align:middle
And those are the things
that we're actually doing.

35:30.906 --> 35:33.316 align:middle
So I don't know that there
are any blind trusts,

35:33.446 --> 35:36.056 align:middle
for that reason, because they
are the, they're the regulator.

35:36.056 --> 35:37.726 align:middle
They say this on their
website, if you look.

35:38.406 --> 35:40.486 align:middle
In terms of laws
broken, you know,

35:40.486 --> 35:44.316 align:middle
I asked the inspector
general to look

35:44.316 --> 35:46.746 align:middle
to see whether there
were rules broken

35:47.076 --> 35:48.396 align:middle
and whether there
were laws broken,

35:48.396 --> 35:49.596 align:middle
and I won't speculate on that.

35:49.626 --> 35:52.596 align:middle
But that is with the
inspector general now and,

35:52.596 --> 35:54.196 align:middle
of course, out of my hands.

35:54.456 --> 35:54.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:56.766 --> 35:57.116 align:middle
Thank you.

35:57.116 --> 35:59.066 align:middle
We'll go to Mike
McKee at Bloomberg.

36:04.686 --> 36:05.066 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

36:05.066 --> 36:09.376 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, the critics of
your patience policy argue that,

36:09.416 --> 36:11.486 align:middle
given the long and variable lags

36:11.486 --> 36:14.446 align:middle
with which monetary policy
works, that you are likely

36:14.446 --> 36:18.536 align:middle
to end up, given inflation, by
having to raise rates faster

36:18.536 --> 36:21.696 align:middle
and farther than you would
have liked and, therefore,

36:21.696 --> 36:24.066 align:middle
send the economy into recession.

36:24.376 --> 36:26.016 align:middle
Given the fact that, basically,

36:26.016 --> 36:29.836 align:middle
your forecast has been chasing
inflation over the last year,

36:29.836 --> 36:32.306 align:middle
and now you're talking about it
not coming down until the second

36:32.306 --> 36:35.986 align:middle
or third quarter, why would
they be wrong in thinking that?

36:36.776 --> 36:37.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:37.496 --> 36:46.446 align:middle
Well, so, let me say what's
happened-and we're very,

36:46.636 --> 36:50.826 align:middle
very straightforward about
it-is that inflation has come

36:50.826 --> 36:52.076 align:middle
in higher than expected,

36:52.856 --> 36:58.786 align:middle
and bottlenecks have been more
persistent and more prevalent.

36:59.516 --> 37:01.136 align:middle
We see that just like
everybody else does,

37:01.136 --> 37:03.116 align:middle
and we see that they're
now on track

37:03.116 --> 37:04.796 align:middle
to persist well into next year.

37:04.916 --> 37:07.876 align:middle
That was not expected-not
expected by us, not expected

37:07.876 --> 37:09.256 align:middle
by other macro forecasters.

37:09.336 --> 37:12.156 align:middle
Now, let me say, you know,
it's difficult enough

37:12.156 --> 37:15.276 align:middle
to just forecast the
economy in normal times.

37:15.536 --> 37:18.946 align:middle
When you're talking about,
you know, global supply chains

37:19.046 --> 37:21.976 align:middle
in turmoil, it's a
whole different thing.

37:22.106 --> 37:25.006 align:middle
And you're talking about a
pandemic that's holding people

37:25.876 --> 37:29.596 align:middle
out of the labor force for
reasons that we can sample,

37:29.596 --> 37:32.236 align:middle
but we don't have a lot
of experience with this.

37:32.236 --> 37:34.166 align:middle
So it's very, very
difficult to forecast

37:34.166 --> 37:36.246 align:middle
and not easy to set policy.

37:36.816 --> 37:38.466 align:middle
So we have to set
policy, though.

37:38.496 --> 37:40.006 align:middle
So that's what we're doing.

37:40.006 --> 37:42.116 align:middle
And, you know, so, to look
at your question this way,

37:42.346 --> 37:44.066 align:middle
I don't think that
we're behind the curve.

37:44.406 --> 37:46.536 align:middle
I actually believe that
policy is well positioned

37:46.536 --> 37:49.136 align:middle
to address the range
of plausible outcomes,

37:49.136 --> 37:51.366 align:middle
and that's what we need to do.

37:51.366 --> 37:53.916 align:middle
I do think it would be
premature to raise rates today.

37:53.916 --> 37:57.076 align:middle
That's not-I don't think
that's controversial.

37:57.076 --> 38:00.186 align:middle
Certainly, I don't know
anyone arguing for that today.

38:00.266 --> 38:03.656 align:middle
And the reason is that there's
still ground to cover to get

38:03.656 --> 38:04.646 align:middle
to maximum employment.

38:04.646 --> 38:08.226 align:middle
And we don't want to stop
that when there's good reason

38:08.226 --> 38:09.646 align:middle
to think-there's still
good reason to think,

38:09.646 --> 38:12.406 align:middle
although it's been delayed,
clearly, there's good reason

38:12.406 --> 38:15.476 align:middle
to think that the economy
will reopen, particularly

38:15.476 --> 38:20.516 align:middle
if we do get past, you know,
significant outbreaks of COVID.

38:20.976 --> 38:23.746 align:middle
That's when we're really going
to see what the characteristics

38:23.746 --> 38:24.926 align:middle
of the labor market are.

38:25.506 --> 38:28.626 align:middle
And, you know, I think,
you know, the bottlenecks

38:28.626 --> 38:31.606 align:middle
that we're seeing in global
supply chains around goods and,

38:31.606 --> 38:33.416 align:middle
frankly, now at our
own domestic ports,

38:33.416 --> 38:35.746 align:middle
because demand is
stronger than the capacity

38:35.746 --> 38:38.556 align:middle
of those ports-those things are
going to work themselves out.

38:38.596 --> 38:39.956 align:middle
We have a flexible economy.

38:40.366 --> 38:44.266 align:middle
It'll take some time, but,
you know, it took-you know,

38:44.266 --> 38:48.076 align:middle
the experts managed to
create a vaccine faster than,

38:48.076 --> 38:49.106 align:middle
certainly, than I expected.

38:49.106 --> 38:50.726 align:middle
And I think this
stuff will work itself

38:50.726 --> 38:51.886 align:middle
out over the course
of next year.

38:51.996 --> 38:55.286 align:middle
That is my baseline
understanding,

38:55.286 --> 38:57.956 align:middle
and that's a very widely
held one among people.

38:57.956 --> 39:02.016 align:middle
But, you know, we are prepared
for different eventualities,

39:02.126 --> 39:04.566 align:middle
and we will use our tools
to achieve price stability

39:04.566 --> 39:05.516 align:middle
and maximum employment.

39:06.176 --> 39:09.236 align:middle
And we're going to let the data
lead us to where we need to go.

39:09.236 --> 39:14.476 align:middle
Our policy will adapt-and has
already adapted-to the changing

39:14.476 --> 39:17.436 align:middle
understanding of
inflation and of bottlenecks

39:17.436 --> 39:20.866 align:middle
and the whole supply-side
story-which is also partly a

39:20.866 --> 39:21.546 align:middle
demand story.

39:22.016 --> 39:25.216 align:middle
So our policy will continue
to adapt as is appropriate.

39:25.486 --> 39:25.826 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:26.986 --> 39:27.656 align:middle
Thank you.

39:27.656 --> 39:30.096 align:middle
Let's go to Nancy
Marshall-Genzer at Marketplace.

39:30.096 --> 39:32.016 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

39:32.156 --> 39:32.926 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

39:32.926 --> 39:34.326 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

39:34.496 --> 39:37.166 align:middle
So you announced that the Fed
is going to taper at a rate of,

39:37.166 --> 39:40.536 align:middle
starting at $15 billion
a month, and that's more

39:40.536 --> 39:43.896 align:middle
than twice the pace
of the last taper.

39:44.266 --> 39:46.866 align:middle
So why are you tapering
faster this time?

39:48.036 --> 39:48.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:48.606 --> 39:50.786 align:middle
The economy is in
quite a different place

39:50.786 --> 39:55.306 align:middle
than when we tapered back
in, I guess it was, 2013.

39:55.616 --> 39:58.826 align:middle
We were much farther away
from maximum employment.

39:59.506 --> 40:01.126 align:middle
Inflation was much lower.

40:01.696 --> 40:05.486 align:middle
This is an economy where
demand is very, very strong,

40:05.676 --> 40:10.386 align:middle
very strong, and job openings
substantially exceed the number

40:10.546 --> 40:11.916 align:middle
of unemployed people.

40:11.916 --> 40:17.936 align:middle
So the need for further stimulus
is far less than it was in 2013,

40:17.936 --> 40:19.536 align:middle
where we still had
quite a ways to go.

40:19.536 --> 40:21.276 align:middle
I mean, after we
began that taper,

40:21.276 --> 40:24.616 align:middle
it was still many years
before we reached what I would

40:24.616 --> 40:26.446 align:middle
characterize as conditions
consistent

40:26.446 --> 40:29.326 align:middle
with maximum employment,
let alone price stability.

40:29.326 --> 40:32.366 align:middle
So this is quite a different
situation, and, you know,

40:32.366 --> 40:35.736 align:middle
the Committee unanimously felt
today that we had met the test

40:35.736 --> 40:37.896 align:middle
that we'd articulated,
and this was appropriate.

40:38.406 --> 40:41.396 align:middle
And this is faster
than, you know,

40:41.396 --> 40:44.776 align:middle
than what people had
expected six months ago.

40:44.776 --> 40:48.506 align:middle
It's earlier and faster,
and that's because our,

40:48.506 --> 40:54.396 align:middle
as I mentioned, our policy has
been adapting to the situation

40:54.396 --> 40:56.566 align:middle
as it evolves, as it's
clarifying itself,

40:56.566 --> 41:00.456 align:middle
and that's partly because we see
inflation coming in higher-so.

41:00.706 --> 41:01.586 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:01.816 --> 41:02.216 align:middle
Thank you.

41:02.566 --> 41:04.856 align:middle
Let's go to Mike Derby.

41:04.856 --> 41:05.416 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

41:05.416 --> 41:10.476 align:middle
Yes, thank you for
taking my question.

41:10.476 --> 41:13.606 align:middle
I wonder if the Fed has given
any thoughts yet to the endgame

41:13.606 --> 41:15.846 align:middle
for the balance sheet
in terms of, you know,

41:15.846 --> 41:17.966 align:middle
once you get the taper
process complete,

41:18.276 --> 41:20.866 align:middle
will you hold the balance sheet
steady, or will you allow it

41:20.866 --> 41:22.596 align:middle
to start passively winding down?

41:22.596 --> 41:26.806 align:middle
And then, in a related question,
do you have any greater insight

41:26.806 --> 41:30.486 align:middle
into what Fed bond buying
actually does for the economy?

41:30.486 --> 41:33.986 align:middle
In terms of its economic impact,
have you been able to, you know,

41:33.986 --> 41:37.266 align:middle
measure it or quantify it
in any fashion, you know,

41:37.266 --> 41:39.936 align:middle
because I'm sure you know
there's often been questions

41:39.936 --> 41:42.126 align:middle
about, what is bond
buying actually doing

41:42.126 --> 41:43.026 align:middle
to help the economy?

41:43.146 --> 41:43.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:43.736 --> 41:45.956 align:middle
Sure. So in terms of the
balance sheet, those questions

41:45.956 --> 41:48.956 align:middle
that you mention-we
haven't gone back to them.

41:49.546 --> 41:50.986 align:middle
Now that we've tapered, I expect

41:50.986 --> 41:53.006 align:middle
that that's exactly what
we'll do in coming meetings.

41:53.006 --> 41:55.606 align:middle
And we'll do it in an orderly
fashion, and we'll talk

41:55.606 --> 41:57.456 align:middle
about reinvestment
and all those things.

41:58.096 --> 42:01.116 align:middle
And we don't have to make
decisions on those yet.

42:01.116 --> 42:03.816 align:middle
But, you know, typically,
when we're doing a new subject

42:03.816 --> 42:06.486 align:middle
like that, we'll have a series
of briefings and discussions.

42:06.816 --> 42:08.986 align:middle
And that's what we
will now begin to do.

42:09.106 --> 42:12.986 align:middle
In terms of the effect of asset
purchases on the economy, so,

42:13.516 --> 42:16.446 align:middle
there's a tremendous
amount of research

42:16.446 --> 42:17.596 align:middle
and scholarship on this.

42:17.596 --> 42:22.816 align:middle
And, you know, you can kind
of-you can find different people

42:22.816 --> 42:23.856 align:middle
coming out with different views.

42:23.856 --> 42:27.416 align:middle
But I would say, the most
mainstream view would be

42:27.416 --> 42:29.516 align:middle
that you're at the
effective lower bound,

42:30.036 --> 42:31.996 align:middle
so how do you affect
longer-term rates?

42:31.996 --> 42:32.896 align:middle
There are two ways.

42:32.976 --> 42:35.876 align:middle
One, so you can't lower
rates-let's say you can't lower

42:35.876 --> 42:37.216 align:middle
rates any further,
hypothetically.

42:37.726 --> 42:39.306 align:middle
So you can give forward
guidance.

42:39.306 --> 42:42.626 align:middle
You can say, we're going to
keep the rates low for a period

42:42.626 --> 42:43.986 align:middle
of time, either [for]
a specific period,

42:43.986 --> 42:45.376 align:middle
[or] until certain
conditions are met.

42:45.856 --> 42:47.996 align:middle
The markets will do the math,
and that'll have an effect

42:47.996 --> 42:50.396 align:middle
on longer-term borrowings
even, you know, 10,

42:50.396 --> 42:51.596 align:middle
30 years out kind of thing.

42:52.126 --> 42:52.776 align:middle
So that's one thing.

42:52.776 --> 42:53.586 align:middle
The other thing you can do is,

42:53.586 --> 42:56.006 align:middle
you just go buy those
securities,

42:56.006 --> 42:57.136 align:middle
buy longer-term securities.

42:57.136 --> 43:01.056 align:middle
That will drive down longer-term
rates and hold them lower, and,

43:01.056 --> 43:02.546 align:middle
you know, rates right
across the,

43:02.686 --> 43:05.446 align:middle
right across the rate
spectrum matter for borrowers.

43:05.446 --> 43:08.376 align:middle
So lower rates encourage
more borrowing,

43:08.376 --> 43:09.946 align:middle
encourage more economic
activity.

43:09.946 --> 43:11.576 align:middle
People-you can service
your debt.

43:11.576 --> 43:12.846 align:middle
You have more free cash flow.

43:13.316 --> 43:14.616 align:middle
You know, it's not different

43:14.616 --> 43:15.836 align:middle
from what we do at
the short end.

43:16.466 --> 43:19.786 align:middle
So that was discussed long
before anybody did it.

43:19.786 --> 43:21.176 align:middle
That was-I think
Milton Friedman said

43:21.176 --> 43:22.836 align:middle
that that was what you
could do if you were pinned

43:22.836 --> 43:25.276 align:middle
at the lower bound,
many, many years ago.

43:25.276 --> 43:27.916 align:middle
So, anyway, that's how
it's supposed to work.

43:27.916 --> 43:31.826 align:middle
And, you know, it's
quite hard to be precise

43:31.826 --> 43:34.386 align:middle
about these things because, you
know, you only have one economy,

43:34.386 --> 43:36.426 align:middle
and you can't run two
different economies right next

43:36.426 --> 43:38.166 align:middle
to each other and do a
scientific experiment.

43:38.166 --> 43:40.616 align:middle
But most people find-most
of the findings are

43:40.616 --> 43:44.136 align:middle
that it does support
economic activity in the way

43:44.136 --> 43:46.636 align:middle
that you would expect, which
is to say, at the margin,

43:47.136 --> 43:49.016 align:middle
more economic activity
with lower rates,

43:49.016 --> 43:50.836 align:middle
which is why we do what we do.

43:50.836 --> 43:53.426 align:middle
More accommodative
financial conditions lead

43:53.426 --> 43:56.446 align:middle
to more economic activity,
over time, with a lag.

43:56.826 --> 44:02.406 align:middle
So I think that's the main
finding I would say on QE.

44:02.466 --> 44:03.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:04.526 --> 44:04.866 align:middle
Thank you.

44:04.866 --> 44:09.786 align:middle
We're going to go to
Paul Lamonica at CNN.

44:09.786 --> 44:10.116 align:middle
PAUL LAMONICA.

44:10.116 --> 44:11.206 align:middle
Chair Powell, Chair Powell,

44:11.206 --> 44:14.166 align:middle
you've addressed
already questions

44:14.166 --> 44:19.216 align:middle
about the stock purchases
that took place from some

44:19.216 --> 44:21.726 align:middle
of the regional Fed
presidents and, you know,

44:21.726 --> 44:24.456 align:middle
addressing the American
people to make sure

44:24.456 --> 44:26.516 align:middle
that they can trust the Fed.

44:26.606 --> 44:30.286 align:middle
I was wondering, also, in light
of the fact that, you know,

44:30.286 --> 44:33.396 align:middle
we now have questions about
your own future, whether

44:33.396 --> 44:37.266 align:middle
or not President Biden will
nominate you for a second term,

44:37.266 --> 44:41.486 align:middle
what would you say-to the
President and to senators that,

44:41.486 --> 44:43.696 align:middle
potentially, you
know, will be voting

44:43.696 --> 44:48.846 align:middle
on a renomination-about this,
specifically with regards

44:48.846 --> 44:52.926 align:middle
to your future as Fed Chair
for a possible second term?

44:52.926 --> 44:54.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:54.206 --> 44:56.576 align:middle
So I'm not going to-I won't have
any-I will answer your question,

44:56.576 --> 44:58.966 align:middle
Paul, but I'm not going to
have any comment whatsoever

44:59.406 --> 45:01.816 align:middle
on the renomination
process at all.

45:01.816 --> 45:02.396 align:middle
PAUL LAMONICA.

45:02.396 --> 45:02.656 align:middle
Fair enough.

45:02.656 --> 45:02.886 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:02.886 --> 45:06.126 align:middle
I will say, though, that I
have briefed Administration

45:06.126 --> 45:11.386 align:middle
officials, and I've briefed
people on Capitol Hill in detail

45:11.386 --> 45:15.166 align:middle
about what we did and why we did
it, and seeking their feedback,

45:15.166 --> 45:16.076 align:middle
getting their reaction.

45:16.076 --> 45:20.666 align:middle
But, you know, this is-part of
my job is-Congress has oversight

45:20.666 --> 45:23.646 align:middle
over the Fed, and we
take that very seriously.

45:23.646 --> 45:27.026 align:middle
So if you're on our, one
of the two committees

45:27.116 --> 45:28.706 align:middle
that has oversight over us,

45:28.706 --> 45:30.856 align:middle
then I'm in regular
contact with you, probably.

45:30.856 --> 45:32.216 align:middle
And, you know, when something

45:32.216 --> 45:33.776 align:middle
like this comes up,
I'm on the phone.

45:33.776 --> 45:35.896 align:middle
I'm offering to meet with
you and explain it to you

45:35.896 --> 45:36.826 align:middle
and answer your questions

45:36.966 --> 45:38.846 align:middle
and identify any concerns
people might have.

45:39.236 --> 45:40.316 align:middle
That's just part of my job.

45:40.676 --> 45:41.726 align:middle
So I do that.

45:41.726 --> 45:43.906 align:middle
I don't talk about
particular conversations.

45:43.906 --> 45:46.086 align:middle
But you can assume
I'll always do that.

45:46.086 --> 45:47.876 align:middle
And I certainly did
it in this case.

45:47.876 --> 45:48.476 align:middle
PAUL LAMONICA.

45:49.756 --> 45:50.176 align:middle
Thank you.

45:50.176 --> 45:50.706 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:50.706 --> 45:50.926 align:middle
Thank you.

45:50.926 --> 45:52.926 align:middle
Let's go to Hannah Lang
at the American Banker.

45:55.636 --> 45:56.006 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

45:56.006 --> 45:59.156 align:middle
Hi. I wanted to ask about the
supplementary leverage ratio.

45:59.156 --> 46:01.606 align:middle
Is the Fed still planning
on taking comment on ways

46:01.606 --> 46:04.286 align:middle
to permanently address that,
and how concerned are you,

46:04.286 --> 46:06.966 align:middle
ultimately, about banks'
willingness to intermediate

46:06.966 --> 46:09.266 align:middle
in the Treasury markets
without a permanent fix?

46:09.776 --> 46:10.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:10.756 --> 46:12.996 align:middle
So I don't have anything for you

46:12.996 --> 46:15.216 align:middle
on supplemental leverage
ratio right now.

46:15.216 --> 46:17.966 align:middle
We are looking at ways to,

46:19.206 --> 46:22.336 align:middle
if there are ways we can
address liquidity issues

46:22.336 --> 46:23.356 align:middle
through that channel.

46:23.886 --> 46:28.546 align:middle
We're also-we also have
a-there's a working group headed

46:28.546 --> 46:32.196 align:middle
by [the] Treasury about, over
Treasury [securities] markets,

46:32.196 --> 46:35.956 align:middle
and what happened in the
acute phase of the pandemic,

46:35.956 --> 46:39.356 align:middle
and what structural
things may need to be done.

46:39.356 --> 46:41.936 align:middle
So that would be part
of that workstream,

46:41.936 --> 46:44.226 align:middle
and I know that there's
a lot going on.

46:44.226 --> 46:46.216 align:middle
I'm not sure when that
report will be out.

46:47.266 --> 46:49.356 align:middle
So it's work under way.

46:49.886 --> 46:52.096 align:middle
That's one of the many
issues that are part of that,

46:52.096 --> 46:55.036 align:middle
along with things like
central clearing of Treasuries,

46:55.036 --> 46:57.866 align:middle
greater central clearing, and,
you know, many other ideas.

46:58.076 --> 47:00.266 align:middle
It's important that we
have a liquid Treasury

47:00.266 --> 47:00.856 align:middle
[securities] market.

47:00.936 --> 47:05.106 align:middle
It's a huge public benefit
that we do, and, you know,

47:05.206 --> 47:09.186 align:middle
I think we need to do those
things that enable that,

47:09.186 --> 47:11.836 align:middle
you know, while also assuring
the safety and soundness

47:11.836 --> 47:13.506 align:middle
of our largest financial
institutions,

47:13.506 --> 47:14.946 align:middle
who tend to be the main dealers.

47:15.686 --> 47:17.246 align:middle
So we have to make sure

47:17.246 --> 47:20.176 align:middle
that that's always a first
order of concern as well.

47:20.176 --> 47:20.636 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

47:20.716 --> 47:22.336 align:middle
Thank you.

47:22.336 --> 47:22.676 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:22.676 --> 47:23.106 align:middle
Thank you.

47:23.106 --> 47:25.046 align:middle
Let's go to Brian
Cheung at Yahoo Finance.

47:25.046 --> 47:25.406 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

47:28.176 --> 47:29.016 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

47:29.016 --> 47:30.076 align:middle
Brian Cheung, Yahoo Finance.

47:30.076 --> 47:32.236 align:middle
So, just to expand on
the ethics conversation,

47:32.236 --> 47:36.076 align:middle
you talked about how you engage
with people on Capitol Hill

47:36.076 --> 47:36.826 align:middle
and in the Administration.

47:36.826 --> 47:38.106 align:middle
You talked about what
you've done already,

47:38.106 --> 47:39.706 align:middle
but I'm just wondering if
you can take a step back

47:39.706 --> 47:43.586 align:middle
and just assess whether or not
there was reputational damage

47:43.586 --> 47:45.696 align:middle
as a result of that, either
from the public's view

47:45.696 --> 47:47.646 align:middle
or from the financial
community's view

47:47.646 --> 47:49.146 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve's
independence.

47:49.146 --> 47:51.656 align:middle
And then, secondly, do you
look back on the whole episode

47:51.656 --> 47:54.346 align:middle
and have thoughts on your
individual responsibility

47:54.346 --> 47:56.786 align:middle
in preventing something like
this from having happened?

47:57.556 --> 48:00.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL You know,
I think it's too soon

48:00.036 --> 48:02.016 align:middle
to say what the reputational
damage is.

48:02.016 --> 48:05.146 align:middle
I think, from the very
beginning, my reaction was:

48:05.146 --> 48:07.076 align:middle
We need to deal with
this straightforwardly,

48:07.076 --> 48:09.166 align:middle
transparently, and forcefully.

48:09.166 --> 48:10.156 align:middle
And that's what we're
going to do.

48:10.156 --> 48:12.996 align:middle
I mean, it's, it
means everything to me

48:12.996 --> 48:16.686 align:middle
that we do whatever it takes
to make sure that nothing

48:16.686 --> 48:17.736 align:middle
like this happens again,

48:17.736 --> 48:21.186 align:middle
and I like to think we've made
a real good start on that.

48:21.186 --> 48:22.066 align:middle
If you think about it,

48:22.066 --> 48:26.256 align:middle
you cannot execute a trade
unless it's precleared,

48:26.666 --> 48:29.576 align:middle
and then you have
to say, "Execute."

48:29.576 --> 48:30.446 align:middle
It's not even a trade.

48:30.446 --> 48:32.636 align:middle
Really, there's no
trading going on.

48:32.636 --> 48:36.696 align:middle
This is for investment
and, you know,

48:36.696 --> 48:38.896 align:middle
getting liquidity
for life's expenses.

48:38.896 --> 48:42.696 align:middle
But you then have to wait
45 days to actually execute

48:42.996 --> 48:44.726 align:middle
that sale or purchase.

48:45.106 --> 48:47.396 align:middle
So I think it's a
pretty good system.

48:47.396 --> 48:50.986 align:middle
We'll always be looking
to make it better.

48:51.586 --> 48:56.956 align:middle
So in terms of our
independence, you know, look,

48:56.956 --> 49:00.296 align:middle
I think we will address
this, and I think we have.

49:00.296 --> 49:04.036 align:middle
And I like to think it's
enough, but it's going to,

49:04.036 --> 49:06.126 align:middle
you know-we're just
beginning to implement it.

49:06.126 --> 49:09.386 align:middle
We have to write the rules,
which we're doing, you know,

49:09.786 --> 49:10.926 align:middle
as quickly as possible.

49:11.236 --> 49:12.146 align:middle
We need more people.

49:12.146 --> 49:15.266 align:middle
We're going to have to resource
this much more significantly

49:15.266 --> 49:16.966 align:middle
here at the Board, and also,

49:16.966 --> 49:20.276 align:middle
we're going to need appropriate
technology, because, you know,

49:20.646 --> 49:23.336 align:middle
the Fed has more than-the System
has more than 30,000 employees.

49:23.336 --> 49:27.356 align:middle
Not all of them, far few of
them, will be covered by this,

49:27.356 --> 49:31.876 align:middle
but the senior officers, who
will be covered by this, will,

49:32.016 --> 49:34.366 align:middle
you know, will have to
have technology access,

49:34.366 --> 49:35.716 align:middle
and it's going to have
to work efficiently.

49:35.716 --> 49:38.406 align:middle
So there's a lot of
work to do to implement.

49:39.106 --> 49:41.346 align:middle
You know, again,
I would just say,

49:41.346 --> 49:45.736 align:middle
this system has been
in place for decades.

49:45.896 --> 49:49.156 align:middle
And it was in place
when I took over.

49:49.806 --> 49:51.336 align:middle
It was in place for the last,

49:51.336 --> 49:52.986 align:middle
at least the last
three or four Chairs.

49:53.306 --> 49:57.066 align:middle
And, you know, it
was what it was.

49:57.066 --> 49:59.946 align:middle
And, you know, it proved
to have weaknesses in it.

49:59.946 --> 50:04.106 align:middle
And part of that was, it
wasn't uniformly enforced

50:04.106 --> 50:05.206 align:middle
across the System.

50:05.206 --> 50:07.546 align:middle
I'm a big believer in the value
of the Federal Reserve System

50:07.546 --> 50:08.346 align:middle
and the Reserve Banks.

50:08.856 --> 50:11.786 align:middle
But you had 12 different ethics
officers at 12 different Banks.

50:11.786 --> 50:13.306 align:middle
And you had ethics people here.

50:13.726 --> 50:15.466 align:middle
And, you know, compliance
wasn't,

50:15.576 --> 50:17.616 align:middle
it wasn't all exactly the same.

50:17.616 --> 50:21.326 align:middle
It was a little bit different
and uneven, and also,

50:21.326 --> 50:22.226 align:middle
the rules were-you know,

50:22.226 --> 50:26.786 align:middle
we didn't imagine the
problems that happened.

50:26.786 --> 50:29.436 align:middle
And they may have actually
been-I don't know this-but they

50:29.436 --> 50:30.566 align:middle
may actually have
been in compliance

50:30.566 --> 50:32.816 align:middle
with the specifics of our rules.

50:32.816 --> 50:34.616 align:middle
They were clearly not in
compliance with the part

50:34.616 --> 50:37.136 align:middle
of our rules that
said, don't do anything

50:37.266 --> 50:40.156 align:middle
that would create
that appearance.

50:40.156 --> 50:41.146 align:middle
I mean, that's clear.

50:41.146 --> 50:42.226 align:middle
This was a bad appearance.

50:42.816 --> 50:46.906 align:middle
So, anyway, what can we do?

50:46.906 --> 50:48.516 align:middle
We are where we are.

50:48.716 --> 50:51.356 align:middle
It happened, and we just have
to deal with it forthrightly

50:51.356 --> 50:55.026 align:middle
and transparently and "own"
it and step up to this,

50:55.026 --> 50:56.546 align:middle
you know, meet this moment.

50:56.546 --> 50:58.766 align:middle
I'm totally committed
to doing that.

50:58.766 --> 51:03.386 align:middle
And, again, if there are better
ideas, I'd love to hear them,

51:03.386 --> 51:06.526 align:middle
but I think we have so
far made a good start.

51:06.756 --> 51:07.456 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

51:07.506 --> 51:11.326 align:middle
When you say you spoke with
Administration officials,

51:11.326 --> 51:12.346 align:middle
did that include the President?

51:14.056 --> 51:14.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

51:14.546 --> 51:17.076 align:middle
I'm not going to answer
who I spoke to at all.

51:17.636 --> 51:21.156 align:middle
I just, I'm not going to give
you any names, so don't take

51:21.156 --> 51:22.016 align:middle
that as a "yes" or "no."

51:22.016 --> 51:23.476 align:middle
I'm just not going to
start down that road.

51:23.886 --> 51:25.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

51:25.246 --> 51:25.906 align:middle
Okay. Thank you.

51:25.906 --> 51:28.136 align:middle
We're going to go to Jeff
Cox for the last question.

51:28.136 --> 51:30.806 align:middle
JEFF COX. Yes, thank you.

51:31.046 --> 51:35.436 align:middle
Chair, I just want to dig a
little bit deeper on employment.

51:35.756 --> 51:40.346 align:middle
We've seen what's been
called the great resignation,

51:40.346 --> 51:45.296 align:middle
folks leaving their
jobs in record numbers.

51:45.406 --> 51:48.866 align:middle
Is there any feeling there
that maybe you've been accused

51:48.866 --> 51:51.806 align:middle
of "fighting the last war"-that
perhaps the labor dynamics have

51:51.806 --> 51:55.506 align:middle
changed in the post-COVID
environment

51:55.506 --> 51:58.606 align:middle
and that full employment
may not look

51:58.606 --> 52:00.466 align:middle
like what it looked like before?

52:00.836 --> 52:02.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:02.756 --> 52:06.486 align:middle
Yes. So, what's happening is,
people are leaving their jobs.

52:06.486 --> 52:10.996 align:middle
They're quitting their jobs
in all-time-high numbers-but,

52:11.586 --> 52:13.586 align:middle
in many cases, going
back into employment

52:13.586 --> 52:14.706 align:middle
and getting higher wages.

52:14.866 --> 52:16.946 align:middle
So a lot of the higher
wages you're seeing are

52:16.946 --> 52:19.046 align:middle
for job switchers
rather than incumbents.

52:19.656 --> 52:23.376 align:middle
So that's just, that's a sign
of a really strong labor market,

52:23.786 --> 52:26.276 align:middle
as opposed to people just
running off and quitting.

52:26.276 --> 52:29.106 align:middle
There have also been-there
are a significant number

52:29.106 --> 52:32.666 align:middle
of retirements, and we'll just
have to see what that means.

52:32.666 --> 52:36.036 align:middle
So, toward the end of the last
cycle, which was the longest

52:36.166 --> 52:38.106 align:middle
in our recorded economic
history,

52:38.756 --> 52:43.656 align:middle
we did see labor force
participation moving up well

52:43.716 --> 52:46.296 align:middle
above what economists
estimate was the trend,

52:46.876 --> 52:49.976 align:middle
and part of that was people
staying in the labor force

52:50.766 --> 52:52.686 align:middle
or just not retiring

52:52.886 --> 52:54.726 align:middle
at the rates they were
expected to retire.

52:55.126 --> 52:57.726 align:middle
So maybe this was
just catch-up on that.

52:57.726 --> 52:59.506 align:middle
I am a believer that, over time,

52:59.866 --> 53:03.356 align:middle
you won't know how far-you
won't know what can happen

53:03.356 --> 53:05.736 align:middle
with labor force
participation in advance.

53:05.936 --> 53:08.356 align:middle
You're just going to have
to give it some time,

53:08.356 --> 53:10.296 align:middle
because we saw that
over and over again.

53:10.296 --> 53:13.306 align:middle
There are things that we can,
where we can say, you know,

53:14.366 --> 53:16.926 align:middle
this is where the limit is.

53:16.926 --> 53:21.356 align:middle
Labor force participation is
a much more flexible subject

53:21.616 --> 53:25.046 align:middle
for me, and so I do think
we need to be humble

53:25.046 --> 53:27.826 align:middle
about what the limits are of
labor force participation.

53:27.826 --> 53:32.106 align:middle
But we expect labor force
participation to pick up.

53:32.656 --> 53:34.566 align:middle
We don't know the pace
at which it will do so.

53:35.046 --> 53:40.246 align:middle
So in terms of full employment,
as I discussed earlier, I think,

53:40.246 --> 53:43.266 align:middle
at the very beginning of the
recovery, the natural thing

53:43.266 --> 53:45.876 align:middle
to do was to look back
at labor conditions,

53:45.876 --> 53:48.356 align:middle
labor market conditions
in February of 2010,

53:48.356 --> 53:50.916 align:middle
at the end of the longest
expansion in our history.

53:50.916 --> 53:53.456 align:middle
There was so much to like
about that labor market,

53:53.456 --> 53:58.046 align:middle
a really historically good
labor market-never perfect,

53:58.096 --> 53:59.086 align:middle
but a good labor market.

53:59.696 --> 54:00.766 align:middle
We're in a different world now.

54:00.766 --> 54:05.346 align:middle
This is a-it's just
very different.

54:06.276 --> 54:09.806 align:middle
The pandemic recession
was the deepest,

54:10.166 --> 54:12.456 align:middle
and the recovery has
been the fastest.

54:13.036 --> 54:15.126 align:middle
And wages didn't really go down.

54:15.496 --> 54:17.996 align:middle
And, you know, real incomes
were more than fully replaced

54:17.996 --> 54:18.976 align:middle
by fiscal policy [support].

54:19.296 --> 54:20.946 align:middle
All of this is completely
unusual.

54:21.366 --> 54:24.146 align:middle
You know, an economy
where inflation was driven

54:24.546 --> 54:28.236 align:middle
by services is now inflation
where-all the inflation is

54:28.236 --> 54:29.926 align:middle
in goods, which have
had negative inflation

54:29.926 --> 54:30.756 align:middle
for a quarter-century.

54:30.756 --> 54:32.776 align:middle
So you ask about
full employment.

54:33.026 --> 54:37.246 align:middle
I think we have to-I'm very open
to the thought that it's going

54:37.246 --> 54:40.346 align:middle
to be an empirical question
of where it is located,

54:40.346 --> 54:42.126 align:middle
and we're just going
to learn more and more.

54:42.126 --> 54:44.956 align:middle
I mean, one thing we'll learn,
I think, I hope we'll learn

54:45.096 --> 54:47.436 align:middle
in the next, in the
near term is,

54:47.966 --> 54:51.486 align:middle
once the Delta variant really
does continue to decline,

54:51.486 --> 54:52.606 align:middle
what's going to happen
to employment?

54:52.606 --> 54:55.616 align:middle
Are we going to start to see
over the winter, you know,

54:55.616 --> 54:57.876 align:middle
significant increases
in jobs again?

54:58.236 --> 54:59.626 align:middle
If you look back,
the three-, six-,

54:59.626 --> 55:01.516 align:middle
and nine-month average
job creation is

55:01.516 --> 55:04.506 align:middle
between 550,000 and 600,000.

55:05.196 --> 55:08.476 align:middle
So if you think of that as
a stronger-you don't have

55:08.476 --> 55:11.636 align:middle
to think back to the million-job
months of June and July.

55:11.636 --> 55:15.656 align:middle
You can just think, okay, 550
to 600-if we should get back

55:15.656 --> 55:18.946 align:middle
on that path, then we would
be making good progress,

55:19.416 --> 55:20.856 align:middle
and we like to see
that, of course.

55:20.856 --> 55:22.326 align:middle
So we'll know so much more.

55:22.366 --> 55:23.776 align:middle
And, believe me, we understand.

55:23.776 --> 55:25.806 align:middle
It's a different
world in so many ways.

55:26.506 --> 55:29.146 align:middle
And we're very open to that.

55:29.146 --> 55:30.886 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

55:31.856 --> 55:34.296 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chair, and thank
you all for joining us today.

55:34.296 --> 55:34.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

55:34.506 --> 55:34.736 align:middle
Thank you.

